00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.900
She is the host of NPR's "Ask Me Another,"

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.410
ladies and gentlemen, Ophira Eisenburg.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.577
(audience applauding)

00:02:00.224 --> 00:02:02.710
- Mwah!

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.543
Eugene.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.860
- I don't know if we
pre-advertised what the title

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.080
of this evening is.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.995
The title this evening is,
"Let's Make America Smart Again."

00:02:15.995 --> 00:02:19.162
(audience applauding)

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.370
So, we combed the land for
people who could give us

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.350
insight into this, and one such person is

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.500
Dr. Professor John Holdren,
the former science advisor

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.225
to President Obama. John
Holdren come on out.

00:02:43.225 --> 00:02:46.392
(audience applauding)

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.870
At the moment, he's Professor
of Environmental Policy

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.692
up at Harvard, and he
came down just for this.

00:02:56.692 --> 00:02:57.880
So thanks John.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:58.921
- Thank you.

00:02:58.921 --> 00:03:00.940
(audience applauding)

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.940
- Also, from the Obama
administration, we have a biologist.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.732
Jo Handelsmen. Jo, come on out.

00:03:08.732 --> 00:03:11.899
(audience applauding)

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.720
She was Associate Director for Science

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.310
at the Office of Science
and Technology Policy

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.610
under President Obama. And
now she's a microbiologist

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.610
at the University of
Wisconsin. Is that right?

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.313
Cheeseheads, yes, yes.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:36.710
So this event will be in three parts.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:40.460
Initially, we'll talk about
Earth, and keeping track

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.630
of what's going on and why.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.410
Talk about the science and
the policy related to that.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:50.410
Next, we will talk about
biology, and how that affects

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.220
health, and get inside the
National Institutes of Health

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.050
and what they're all about and why.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.350
And we'll end up with a
final segment on the future.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.900
The future of space, the
future of AI, robotics,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.360
and so we're gonna do it all.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.420
All the science that
matters in this country,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:08.740
and we're doing it now.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.760
This is a live broadcast, here,
at the Count Basie Theater

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.877
Red Bank, New Jersey, StarTalk.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.291
Let's do this!

00:04:17.291 --> 00:04:20.458
(audience applauding)

00:04:24.036 --> 00:04:29.036
Oh right. Well, Earth Day is
in April. Earth Day, April 22

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.256
and it coincides with the
Science March on Washington.

00:04:34.256 --> 00:04:36.940
(audience applauding)

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.860
So John, Earth Day began in 1970.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:41.693
- Right.

00:04:41.693 --> 00:04:42.555
- Why?

00:04:42.555 --> 00:04:43.388
Why not 1960?

00:04:43.388 --> 00:04:45.830
Surely people cared about Earth in 1960.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.090
- No, people did. But what
had happened during the course

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.360
of the '60s is there were the whole series

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.474
of environmental
disasters that got people-

00:04:52.474 --> 00:04:54.350
(Neil laughs)

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.650
- So we need disaster to protect.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.590
So we don't know how to
protect something proactively.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:00.845
- Disasters help.

00:05:00.845 --> 00:05:02.040
- Yep.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.467
- What were like the three best disasters?

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:05.810
(laughing)

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.923
- The Cuyahoga River
catching fire was one-

00:05:09.923 --> 00:05:11.423
- That'll do it, yeah.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.190
- Lake Eerie becoming
totally clogged with algae,

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.330
so that most of the fish were
dying was the second one.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:19.570
- I can see-
- This one sounds worse.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.120
- In that period. And of course,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.960
the air pollution in the Los Angeles Basin

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.270
getting worse and worse, so
that on most days you couldn't

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:26.310
see the mountains.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.890
In fact, I was at Cal
Tech in the early 1970s-

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:30.723
- In Pasadena?

00:05:30.723 --> 00:05:32.970
- In Pasadena, I had
been there for six months

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.957
before I knew there were
mountains right behind Cal Tech.

00:05:35.957 --> 00:05:37.480
(laughing)

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.670
- Okay, so Jo, you
worked with John Holdren

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.493
in the Office of Science
and Technology Policy,

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:46.330
what is OSTP?

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.280
I'd say most people
have never heard of it.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:49.421
So why?

00:05:49.421 --> 00:05:51.875
What did you not do?

00:05:51.875 --> 00:05:52.708
(laughter)

00:05:52.708 --> 00:05:55.543
- Well, we obviously didn't
advertise ourselves very much.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.230
Our job was to mix policy and science.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.520
And that meant two things,
some of it was policy

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.420
for science, how to make
our science enterprise

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.140
as strong as it could be,
using policy to shape it.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:12.320
But the other side was using
science to shape policy

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.252
on issues that weren't
obviously about science.

00:06:15.252 --> 00:06:16.090
- Like what?

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.960
- Like forensics, forensic
science is supposedly

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.260
based on science, but in fact there's not

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.910
that much science behind it.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.264
And so, we brought the
science to bear on that issue.

00:06:27.264 --> 00:06:31.483
- And so John, you were appointed
by Obama, is that correct?

00:06:31.483 --> 00:06:32.316
- Yes.

00:06:32.316 --> 00:06:33.980
- Did you have to be
approved by the Senate?

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.820
- Yes, but there are two
different jobs involved.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.440
Science Advisor to the
president is not subject

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.210
to Senate confirmation,
Director of the White House

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.400
Office of Science and Technology Policy,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.662
is subject to-
- Didn't you have both titles?

00:06:47.662 --> 00:06:50.710
- And I had both titles,
but I could start serving

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:53.504
as the president's Science
Advisor on Inauguration Day

00:06:53.504 --> 00:06:58.040
2009, took two months to get
confirmed as Director of OSTP.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.320
And in those two months, I
couldn't sit in the director's

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.390
office, couldn't give any
orders to anybody in OSTP,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.480
but I could talk to the President.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:06.313
- Wow.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.284
- Glad this bureaucracy's
gonna be gone now.

00:07:10.284 --> 00:07:12.088
(laughter)

00:07:12.088 --> 00:07:16.180
- So, how was Obama among the presidents

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.730
with regard to science, would you say?

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:22.250
- Well, I think President Obama
was the most science savvy

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.394
president since Thomas Jefferson.

00:07:24.394 --> 00:07:27.561
(audience applauding)

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.910
Thomas Jefferson, of course,
was his own Science Advisor.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.010
- Yeah, he was badass.

00:07:34.872 --> 00:07:37.359
- I hope Warren Harding
never hears you say this.

00:07:37.359 --> 00:07:39.493
(panel laughs)

00:07:39.493 --> 00:07:43.210
- But the president came
into office understanding

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.350
how and why science and
technology matter for the economy,

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.480
for public health, for the environment,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.426
for national security, he just got it.

00:07:50.426 --> 00:07:51.455
- So he was pre-loaded?

00:07:51.455 --> 00:07:53.790
- He was pre-loaded, he just got it.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:54.640
He understood it.

00:07:55.525 --> 00:07:59.480
- All right, so Earth
Day, I still, forgive me,

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:02.460
don't really know what you're
supposed to do on Earth Day.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.270
Like, what're you supposed to do?

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.920
- I think you're supposed to
draw a picture of the Earth.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:09.753
- Yeah.

00:08:09.753 --> 00:08:10.853
- And then get it on a tote bag.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:13.040
- Yeah and walk around with it.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.220
- Walk on the Earth.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.053
- Appreciate it.

00:08:15.053 --> 00:08:15.980
- Walk on the Earth.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.600
- Yeah, touch the Earth.
You know, that'd be

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.000
a good thing on Earth Day.
- Eat a stick of pot butter.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.840
- Yeah, very common.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.705
- Look at a baby for as
long as it'll let you.

00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:27.471
(audience laughing)

00:08:27.471 --> 00:08:29.521
(scattered chatter)

00:08:29.521 --> 00:08:33.938
- Now, Earth Day has a
biological motive, doesn't it?

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.223
- Absolutely.

00:08:36.223 --> 00:08:38.920
Some of us think it should
be renamed Soil Day,

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.510
because it's about the
Earth, but the most important

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.370
thing on the Earth is the soil.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:45.850
That's where all of our
food comes from, and--

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.250
- Wait a minute, don't you study soil?

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.277
- I do, I'm a little biased.

00:08:50.277 --> 00:08:52.390
- I was like, that's very specific.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.589
- How many wanna call it Soil Day?

00:08:54.589 --> 00:08:55.456
- Yeah.

00:08:55.456 --> 00:08:56.956
- Um, a few of us.

00:08:58.501 --> 00:08:59.790
- I feel like water is pretty important.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.310
- Yeah, that's true, yeah. But soil--

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:07.190
- See, I could go more
than a week without soil.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:09.480
(laughter)

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.027
- Keep telling yourself that.

00:09:11.027 --> 00:09:12.550
- I can't go 10 days without water.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:16.060
- But the Earth is dead
really fast without soil.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.730
So, you can't use yourself
as the standard, sorry.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:20.740
But it's okay.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.830
- And Neil, you can't go
for a week without air.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:25.275
Air is really important.

00:09:25.275 --> 00:09:26.108
- True.

00:09:26.108 --> 00:09:27.911
- Yeah, that's minutes without air.

00:09:27.911 --> 00:09:31.890
So when I think of Earth Day 1970,

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.240
in that same year, the EPA,
Environmental Protection Agency

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.600
was founded, and so was NOAA,

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.407
The National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration.

00:09:40.407 --> 00:09:41.240
(audience cheering)

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.073
- So these are--

00:09:42.073 --> 00:09:42.906
- NOAA fan.

00:09:42.906 --> 00:09:46.760
- That was a benchmark
year for people caring.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.920
So, I'm trying to understand.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.918
- Well, on a slightly more
serious note one of the things--

00:09:52.918 --> 00:09:54.713
- More serious than
caring about the Earth?

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.480
- No, more serious than you're going

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.726
for a week without soil.

00:09:58.726 --> 00:09:59.559
- Oh right, okay.

00:09:59.559 --> 00:10:00.550
(laughter)

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.900
- On a more serious note, what
it's really about I think,

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.090
is talking with folks
and reminding them about

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:10.140
all the ways we depend on air, and soil,

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:12.180
and water, and sunlight.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.550
And the trouble is, most
people today, too many people,

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:20.670
think that food materializes
de novo on supermarket shelves.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.440
You know, they think that
when they plug something

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.330
into the wall, the electricity
is coming from right

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:25.706
behind the wall,

00:10:25.706 --> 00:10:26.539
(scattered chatter)

00:10:26.539 --> 00:10:28.647
and that there's a whole
system connected to it.

00:10:28.647 --> 00:10:30.710
And they think that most pest control

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.280
is done by pesticides,
they think that most

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.680
availability of water is
through canals and dams,

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:36.950
they just don't get it.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.690
That we depend on the
Earth for our well-being.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:40.760
And that's what it's really about.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.980
- You guys worked in an office,

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:47.180
that should be household
conversation, and it's not.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.260
How come?

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.480
- Well I think partly 'cuz
we backed up the president.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.820
The president was the
face of OSTP in many ways,

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:57.240
because he rolled out the
policy, he represented it--

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.630
- I remember, when was
it, when C. Everett Koop

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:04.300
told people how to use
condoms, or whatever,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.825
I forgot the details, as,
what was his title again?

00:11:07.825 --> 00:11:08.658
(laughter)

00:11:08.658 --> 00:11:09.491
No, I forgot!

00:11:09.491 --> 00:11:12.253
It's not about sex, it's not about sex.

00:11:12.253 --> 00:11:14.080
- I believe he told people to masturbate

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:16.195
and then the president
had to take it back .

00:11:16.195 --> 00:11:18.806
(laughter)

00:11:18.806 --> 00:11:19.730
- I'm not totally wrong.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:21.237
- No, no that was the next--

00:11:23.914 --> 00:11:24.830
- That was Madeline Albright?

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.250
- Joycelyn Elders.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.420
- Right, Joycelyn Elders.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.650
- I remember the
masturbation lessons, yeah.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.400
- Why do I even know his name then?

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.682
- So he was around, I
think, in the AIDs era.

00:11:36.682 --> 00:11:38.590
So we all new his name.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:39.423
- Yeah, yeah.

00:11:39.423 --> 00:11:43.970
- And so, so why didn't we know your name?

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.300
- 'Cuz you were out of it,
you must've been out of it.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:49.520
Everybody I know knows
John Holdren's name.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.190
- You work with the guy.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.750
- Well, but I know a few
people who are outside

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.810
the White House, and they
all think that he's a hero.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:57.643
- So here's the thing--

00:11:57.643 --> 00:11:59.730
(applause)

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:04.490
So Earth Day, there is somehow in the air,

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:09.090
no pun intended, a sense of
concern for Earth as a planet.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:10.690
That came out, I think it
was right in the middle

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:13.064
of when we were going to the moon, right?

00:12:13.064 --> 00:12:13.897
- Mhmm.

00:12:13.897 --> 00:12:16.530
- But I think we went to explore the moon,

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:19.993
we looked back, and discovered
Earth for the first time.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.880
And in that discovery, Earth
became a focus of our concern

00:12:29.038 --> 00:12:31.120
and we want to preserve
this spaceship floating

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:32.540
there in the void.

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.290
- We basically took a planet-sized selfie.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.230
- Yeah, basically. That's right.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.900
From a quarter million miles away.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:43.690
So it looks like we exploited
that fact legislatively.

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:49.000
- Absolutely. That blue
marble photo, that selfie,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.610
that Baratonde has just
referred to, was critical.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.930
- And so, EPA gets formed.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.460
Apparently, not under any
controversy, right? I guess.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:01.610
- Well, you know it was
interesting that, you know

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:04.258
President Nixon actually thought it was

00:13:04.258 --> 00:13:05.170
important to do that.

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.920
- And I think he was
Republican, last I remembered.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.150
- It's true.
(laughter)

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:12.050
It was one of his better moments.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:13.630
- Okay.
(laughter)

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.890
- I think today he'd be
considered a communist.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.140
(laughter)

00:13:20.107 --> 00:13:25.107
- So the mission of the EPA,
to ensure that all Americans

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.840
are protected from risks to human health

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.660
and to the environment,
from where they live,

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:32.513
where they learn, and where they work.

00:13:33.381 --> 00:13:38.061
So, this sounds like an
important organization.

00:13:38.061 --> 00:13:40.300
- A very important
organization, and one which has

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:43.550
been built up since its
inception, and which has done

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.700
on the whole, a pretty good job.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:50.100
- And what's the relationship
between NOAA and the EPA?

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:53.250
- Well NOAA is basically
an environmental monitoring

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.590
and a science organization.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.320
They're responsible for
understanding what's happening

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.520
in the oceans, what's
happening in the atmosphere.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:02.170
The National Weather Service
is part of NOAA, for example.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.510
So our weather forecasts come from NOAA.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:08.047
And by the way, a member of
Congress once famously said,

00:14:08.047 --> 00:14:10.227
"I don't know why we have
to fund NOAA, we have

00:14:10.227 --> 00:14:11.569
"The Weather Channel."

00:14:11.569 --> 00:14:13.212
(laughter)

00:14:13.212 --> 00:14:14.045
- Idiot.

00:14:14.045 --> 00:14:16.646
- And that's what's called
unclear on the concept.

00:14:16.646 --> 00:14:18.896
(laughter)

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.080
- All right. So, both
organizations exist today.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:29.223
And can you comment on
their support in Congress?

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.720
- Well it's mixed, obviously.
NOAA and EPA have had

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.570
strong support through both
Republican and Democratic

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:40.340
administrations over the years.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:41.173
- Right.

00:14:41.173 --> 00:14:46.173
- But now, they are faced
with severe budget cuts,

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:50.280
most severe in EPA, if
President Trump's budget

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:51.510
is accepted by Congress,

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.470
and that's not a foregone conclusion.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:56.340
He's proposed a budget
fo EPA, but Congress

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:57.173
has to approve it.

00:14:57.173 --> 00:15:02.173
But that budget cuts EPA's
total funding by over 30%

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:05.430
and the research and
development organization

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:09.398
in EPA is cut by 50% in
that proposed budget.

00:15:09.398 --> 00:15:13.550
NOAA's budget is also being
cut, particularly in respect

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:17.180
to ocean monitoring and
research and climate data.

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:18.332
- Now, you don't have
anything to do with that,

00:15:18.332 --> 00:15:19.600
'cuz you're not in Washington anymore.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.120
- That's right.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.109
- So why am I even
asking you this question?

00:15:23.109 --> 00:15:23.942
(laughter)

00:15:23.942 --> 00:15:25.860
- Well a lot of people are
asking me what is going

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:27.564
to be the role of science and technology

00:15:27.564 --> 00:15:29.194
in the new administration--

00:15:29.194 --> 00:15:30.900
- I'm not gonna ask you
that, I wanna ask that

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:35.347
of a politician. Is there
a politician in the house?

00:15:37.141 --> 00:15:38.474
(laughter)

00:15:38.474 --> 00:15:39.539
- He looked like one.

00:15:39.539 --> 00:15:40.744
- He does look like one.

00:15:40.744 --> 00:15:42.013
- Oh!

00:15:42.013 --> 00:15:45.346
(cheering and applause)

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.070
- Hello Red Bank!

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.800
- Senator Cory Booker!

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.333
New Jersey.

00:15:59.333 --> 00:16:02.666
(cheering and applause)

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.911
- So you can't come up
in my house, Jersey,

00:16:08.911 --> 00:16:11.510
and not say hello, man.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.200
I got word that you were in Jersey.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:14.033
- You did get word.

00:16:14.033 --> 00:16:16.110
- You crossed from the
dark side of the Hudson.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:17.120
- I crossed the moat.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:17.953
- Into the light.

00:16:17.953 --> 00:16:19.490
- I crossed the moat of the Hudson River.

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:21.260
- Welcome to New Jersey,
it's great to have you.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:23.846
- Thank you, I'm delighted
to be in your home state.

00:16:23.846 --> 00:16:26.096
(applause)

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.490
Senator Brooker, you're
former mayor of Newark.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:31.350
- Yes.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.090
- That can't have been easy.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.360
- It was the best
hardest years of my life.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:36.193
- There you go.

00:16:36.193 --> 00:16:39.640
And so, you are sitting
senator in midterm right now,

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:40.473
correct?

00:16:40.473 --> 00:16:41.306
- Thank you New Jersey.

00:16:41.306 --> 00:16:42.250
- Yes, okay.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:46.490
So we have people who previously served,

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.380
and you were in Congress
while they were serving.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:50.880
- First of all, their names
might not be publicly known,

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:53.800
but they are heroic people that made Obama

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:55.390
probably one of the
greatest science presidents

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:57.364
we've ever had, due to
the people he had around.

00:16:57.364 --> 00:16:59.679
- The folks he put in place.

00:16:59.679 --> 00:17:01.223
- Yes.

00:17:01.223 --> 00:17:04.460
- And so, we're now talking
about a president's budget.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:06.670
'Cuz a lot of this I
think is just a mystery

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.040
to so many people, and
certainly a mystery to me.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:12.210
So the president puts out a budget,

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:16.040
and doesn't the Congress
kinda have to go along

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.483
with most of what that is?
- Not at all, not at all.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.280
The Article I branch of the Constitution,

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:23.440
the first branch of government described

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:24.600
is the Congress.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.470
They have extraordinary
powers, they set the budget.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.900
So the president suggests
or presents something

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:33.290
to Congress, but Congress
actually makes the decisions.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:34.960
- That is like a
constitutional smack down.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:36.992
That was so politely delivered.

00:17:36.992 --> 00:17:38.060
(Cory laughs)

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.763
Like Article I, son, suggests actually.

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:44.910
- By the way, we could
do the Hamilton too.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:46.200
Article I, son.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:49.060
Almost had to like restyle it right now.

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.410
- We'll round it backstage.

00:17:51.374 --> 00:17:54.400
- So you and your fellow
99 other senators actually

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:55.980
yield real power on that budget.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.380
- It's extraordinary
power, extraordinary power.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.001
- I'm happy to hear that, honestly.

00:18:02.001 --> 00:18:03.345
(laughter)

00:18:03.345 --> 00:18:05.760
I'm happy, let's hear more. We have power.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.130
- I was aghast at the president's budget.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.440
I think it was one of
the most scary documents

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.700
that basically put forth in
one document a reflection

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:14.340
of what his values are.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.670
Which frankly, if you look at his budget,

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:18.980
the so called Skinny
Budget, and the way he tears

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:21.690
apart critical programs
that affect every element

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.710
of American's lives, even
the base of the people

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.940
that voted for him, it
was a patent betrayal

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.000
of those people who supported
him. And would, in terms

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.740
of creating jobs and
economic strength is what he

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:36.810
preached in his campaigning
days, it would have

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:39.390
really debilitated this
nation's ability to compete

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:43.780
globally in a world that now
is a knowledge based economy.

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.280
Which means science, and
innovation, and technology is

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.960
so critical. So to take
away the government's role

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:51.550
in that would really be
putting both hands behind

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:53.657
our back as we're
competing with the Chinese

00:18:53.657 --> 00:18:55.950
and the German and the
Japanese, who are making

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:58.240
significant investments.
In fact, beginning to

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.400
outstrip America when
it comes to investing

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:01.233
in R&amp;D.

00:19:03.689 --> 00:19:06.980
- So I wanna compare sort of
what were some of the successes

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:11.970
under EPA and NOAA in Obama
that might be at risk right now?

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:13.610
- Well, I just wanna
let people know, I mean

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:17.040
everything we touch represents
your public dollars.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.260
I mean, everything here,
from the batteries,

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:22.530
to the touch screen,
to the GPS. The origin

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.030
of all this science and
technology that the private sector

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:27.868
is now using to create thousands of jobs

00:19:27.868 --> 00:19:32.050
is public sector investments
in science technology.

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.010
Hardly anybody knows that,
wholeheartedly knows that.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:37.020
Dollar investment, in
fact all of us probably,

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:38.320
are fiscal conservatives.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:39.990
I had to be when I was
the mayor of Newark.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.410
Every tax payer dollar was precious.

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:45.190
The reality is, is the
best return on investment

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:48.190
for a tax payer dollar, one
of the best ones you could get

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.430
in government investments
is in things like

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:52.340
The National Institute of Health,

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.170
in such things investing in science.

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:56.800
You get almost more than double the return

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:59.670
in terms of long term economic
growth for our economy.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.380
And so to savage those
programs, to cut the EPA--

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.170
- So they're people who don't recognize

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:08.380
that the government is
actually a good place

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.300
where some kinds of money gets spent.

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:11.860
Rather than saying the
government should have

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:13.410
no money at all.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:14.930
- Yeah, well let's just
take a step back and just

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:16.290
look at the EPA.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:20.250
I mean, I believe in the
principles of a free market

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.490
but Newark, for example, their
environment is a testimony

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:24.900
to the free market run amuck.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.970
In other words, the river,
Passaic River is toxic,

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:30.230
the soil in Newark is full of lead,

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.590
the oxygen, we have terrible asthma rates.

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:35.690
And those were caused by
companies that weren't

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.900
properly regulated, pouring
toxins into the world

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:41.830
and destroying, not just the
health of the environment,

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:44.590
but also the fiscal
competitiveness of the city

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:45.423
in the long term.

00:20:45.423 --> 00:20:47.683
So by the EPA being savaged like it is,

00:20:47.683 --> 00:20:50.210
it's not just the research
and development side,

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:52.380
but just holding people
accountable for the laws

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.330
that they're breaking out there
and hurting the environment

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:56.400
actually costs all of us money.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.350
- So, this is a message that
needs to get out there, okay?

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:03.000
So you're a senator,
who's elected to office,

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.690
and you don't have a
formal science background,

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:06.550
so who's gonna listen--

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.647
- Whoa, whoa, whoa. I have a
degree in Political Science.

00:21:09.647 --> 00:21:11.897
(laughter)

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:17.503
- Got me there, okay.

00:21:18.906 --> 00:21:20.268
- That was horrible.

00:21:20.268 --> 00:21:22.640
- So there are advisors,

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:24.310
there's the National Academy of Sciences,

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.490
Office of Science Technology and Policy,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:30.850
so we would like to think
that when they speak,

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:34.530
the public listens and heeds.

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:36.370
But somehow that's not happening,

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.540
and I don't think any
of us understand why.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.440
So what's up with that?

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.110
(laughter)

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:45.010
- Well, I think that
it is really important,

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:46.760
now more than ever, and I
think that a lot of folks

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:48.880
learned this with this last election,

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:52.270
that the only thing necessary
for evil to be triumphant

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:53.990
is for good people to do nothing.

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:57.382
- Or to do like some, but not enough.

00:21:57.382 --> 00:22:00.740
- Yes.
(applause)

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:02.740
- Right, we've learned
that re-tweeting is just

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:03.631
not gonna cut it.

00:22:03.631 --> 00:22:06.423
(laughter)
- Depends on the tweet though.

00:22:06.423 --> 00:22:08.760
Some of those tweets are hot fire.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.390
- Yes, and so the age old
wisdom which I just cited,

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:15.450
the reality is, is we did not
have an activist citizenry.

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.380
And a lot of folks, and
we were joking before

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:22.280
about this concept of love,
but patriotism by definition

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:25.550
is love of country. Love
is not a beaten word,

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:27.990
it demands action and sacrifice.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:29.750
So if you love your
country, you've gotta stay

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:32.350
engaged on these issues, you've gotta stay

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:35.950
involved, and you've gotta let
your elected officials know

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:37.780
that, hey you're gonna
be overseeing a budget,

00:22:37.780 --> 00:22:41.450
in fact, the budget of this
country runs out on April 28.

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.730
And the budget decisions
are gonna be made probably

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:45.020
on a continuing resolution.

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.410
What're you gonna prioritize?

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:49.200
If you're not speaking up and
letting your voice be heard,

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.850
and engaging in the process,
then you're basically

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:53.713
surrendering precious space like the one

00:22:53.713 --> 00:22:55.010
that we're just talking about.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:59.330
- So let me just, anchor this in data.

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:01.700
So in order to make an informed decision,

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.460
make informed protest even,

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:07.760
you need data. And NOAA is responsible

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:09.830
for many many satellites that orbit

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:12.690
around the Earth monitoring
climate, for example.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.240
And you would think, that
this would be sufficient

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.720
so that people will then
hear about the data,

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.170
learn about the data,
and act upon the data,

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:24.200
as citizen scientists, if you will.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:27.760
So, where is the disconnect here?

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:32.450
- Well first of all, the
public actually understands

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.640
climate change better than many
members of the Congress do.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.980
(applause)

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:39.416
- He's right.

00:23:39.416 --> 00:23:40.882
- Got a lot of snaps for that one,

00:23:40.882 --> 00:23:42.822
lot of snaps for that one.

00:23:42.822 --> 00:23:44.300
- Sorry Cory.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:47.160
- He's no longer working
in the government now,

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:48.550
so he can say that.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:49.920
- No, but it's true.
That's what polls show.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.590
You know, polls show, and I said this

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:53.980
to President Obama at one point,

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:56.500
polls show that in the range of two-thirds

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:59.120
of Americans believe that
climate change is real,

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:01.920
substantially caused by
humans, already doing harm,

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:03.940
we need to do something
about it. Two-thirds.

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:06.783
By the way, only 50%
believe evolution is a fact.

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:10.113
And when I told the president that,

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:12.987
and he was saying, "You scientists have to

00:24:12.987 --> 00:24:14.970
"do a better job educating the public."

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:17.177
And I said, "Well we've done
pretty well on climate change,

00:24:17.177 --> 00:24:18.597
"and it's more than evolution."

00:24:18.597 --> 00:24:20.540
And he said, "That's no consolation."

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.480
(laughter)

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:23.700
- So, it's not 100%.

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:27.340
So people, they seem to be

00:24:29.664 --> 00:24:31.940
sort of in denial of data

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:34.280
that they don't like.

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:36.530
- Well, it's almost like they're afraid

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:37.970
of what the data is going to say.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:40.750
I mean, we have laws in Congress
that were shocking to me

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:43.160
that I found out, where
we're blocking even

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:45.880
studying things. Like we're blocking even

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:48.300
the studying of gun
violence, and understanding

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:49.430
the effects of it.

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:50.670
And so--
(applause)

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:52.960
- The supporters of blocking gun violence.

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:53.793
- Yeah, yeah.

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:57.730
And so the question is why?

00:24:57.730 --> 00:24:59.823
And I wanna be very blunt
with some of the things

00:24:59.823 --> 00:25:01.400
that we have to understand.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:03.470
There are large, moneyed interests,

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:05.560
large corporate interest in this country,

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:08.930
that are very invested into
the status quo right now.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:12.760
And on elections, they
spend billions and billions

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:17.210
of dollars, supporting
people who will protect

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:20.520
fossil fuel industries and
others, protect the status quo.

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:23.830
So you can't expect, with
that much money being poured

00:25:23.830 --> 00:25:25.660
into the system, that the
people that often get elected

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.920
as a result of that,
won't be protecting that

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:29.160
despite the evidence.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:31.230
And try to do everything they
can to fight the evidence.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.410
Like the tobacco industry for so long.

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:35.510
They would fund scientists
who would come up with

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:38.240
funky science that was
wrong, and try to debunk,

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:39.890
or at least confuse people as to what the

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:42.100
data was showing about cigarette smoking.

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:47.100
- So, Jo, you for a while
worked in infectious diseases.

00:25:47.150 --> 00:25:49.970
Is that right? Or you
were part of the programs

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:51.930
that taught people about it?

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:55.890
- Yeah, in OSTP we dealt
with several epidemics,

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:59.730
like Ebola and Zika
virus, and we handled it.

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:02.660
John and I were the key people in OSTP

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:03.493
and we handled it.

00:26:03.493 --> 00:26:06.550
- So, that's a case where,
if something goes wrong,

00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:07.960
people get sick and die.

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:08.793
- Right.

00:26:08.793 --> 00:26:10.750
- So there's an immediate
cause and effect.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:11.583
- Yep.

00:26:11.583 --> 00:26:13.420
- And climate change has a little bit more

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:16.580
of a horizon, but if sea levels
rise and you start flooding,

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:18.930
seems to me that's cause and effect.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.060
- Well, when heat waves,
and droughts, and wild fires

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:23.790
burning larger and larger areas.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:24.740
You know, in the Artic.

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:26.750
- Can't this weigh more than the billions

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:27.990
of dollars for advertising?

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:29.770
- I think we might be lookin'
at this the wrong way.

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:31.640
First of all, funky
science sounds amazing,

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:32.902
I don't know why you're dissing it.

00:26:32.902 --> 00:26:33.735
(laughter)

00:26:33.735 --> 00:26:35.720
I wanna go to a funky science party.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:40.110
Second, John you started
off, like the EPA and NOAA

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:42.360
were born out of rivers on fire.

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:44.240
Maybe they're trying
to return to that level

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.820
of disaster and urgency to inspire us.

00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:48.033
- No, it's happening.

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:50.653
- That's good.

00:26:50.653 --> 00:26:53.483
- The reason that so many
Americans now believe

00:26:53.483 --> 00:26:56.020
that climate change is real and dangerous

00:26:56.020 --> 00:26:58.670
is they're experiencing it in their lives,

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:00.750
they're seeing it on their TV sets,

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:04.150
they're seeing it on their iPads.

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:08.640
And it's stunning, if
you look at the expansion

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:12.020
of the areas afflicted by
extreme heat every summer.

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.720
If you look at the areas
burned by wild fires.

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:19.500
For the first time, in modern
times, the Tundra is burning

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:21.850
in the Arctic. The Tundra is burning,

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:23.410
that never happened before.

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:24.823
- That sounds terrible.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:27.640
- It never happened before in the period

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:28.713
when we were looking.

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:33.080
- I went to a doctor for a sinus cold,

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:34.750
and she told me it was global warming,

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:37.517
I swear to you. She said
it was global warming.

00:27:37.517 --> 00:27:38.810
- She sounds like a kook.

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:40.003
- I did report her.

00:27:40.875 --> 00:27:41.708
- You've seen every kook.

00:27:41.708 --> 00:27:46.708
- But New Jersians are
seeing the financial impact

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:49.840
of climate change right now.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:51.400
Most people don't
understand, we have a massive

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:53.160
fishing industry in New Jersey.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.330
What's happening with the
acidification of our oceans,

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:57.480
the warming of the oceans,
they're seeing fish

00:27:57.480 --> 00:27:58.810
that they used to be able
to find off the coast

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:00.380
of New Jersey are now being found

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:03.830
further up in Connecticut and Maine.

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:05.730
- That's kind of nice for New England.

00:28:07.245 --> 00:28:09.490
- But New England is complaining

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:11.040
because they're seeing
lobsters and other things

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:12.895
moving further and further to Canada.

00:28:12.895 --> 00:28:13.728
- That is a problem.

00:28:13.728 --> 00:28:16.150
- And so, but more than
that, we are a state

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:17.630
that lives in flood areas.

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:21.570
And now, the flood maps,
literally, now you're seeing

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.650
what used to be hundred
year floods happening

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:26.060
with more frequency, which
is costing New Jersians

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:27.030
a lot more money.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:29.380
- Okay, so I like what you were saying,

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:34.290
who said it, that maybe
we're returning to the

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:39.040
pre-1970 state of circumstances

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:43.550
where we've gotta drop low
before we recover as one.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:45.430
- They're tryna inspire us man.

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:47.166
- Oh, the disasters are
trying to inspire us?

00:28:47.166 --> 00:28:49.730
Oh, that's an interesting
way to think about it, yeah.

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:51.307
- It's the only way to stay sane.

00:28:51.307 --> 00:28:52.517
(laughter)

00:28:52.517 --> 00:28:55.300
- So do you think maybe
it would help to just

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:57.187
light a few local rivers on fire?

00:28:57.187 --> 00:28:59.612
(laughter)

00:28:59.612 --> 00:29:00.765
- That's what I'm sayin'.

00:29:00.765 --> 00:29:03.200
- Like, do we need to
light rivers that Trump

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:04.460
would come across?

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:05.680
Like on his walks?

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.223
- Like Mar-a-Lago. Light
Mar-a-Lago on fire.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:12.236
- Fifth river at my
golf course is on fire.

00:29:12.236 --> 00:29:14.486
(applause)

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:19.450
- So Jo, is it too late?

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:22.190
- No, I don't think it's
too late, and I think the--

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:26.420
- Because the river on fire,
that's all kinda local stuff.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:28.086
- Right. And when we talk
about climate change,

00:29:28.086 --> 00:29:29.660
we're talking about planet wide.

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:30.493
- Yep.

00:29:30.493 --> 00:29:32.260
- So that requires planet wide

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:34.630
cooperation and participation.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:37.730
So is it too late?

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:39.470
- Well, there was one study that showed

00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:42.800
that people believe in
climate change based

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:46.810
more on the three previous days'
weather than anything else.

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:48.394
- Wow.

00:29:48.394 --> 00:29:49.438
- People are idiots.

00:29:49.438 --> 00:29:51.150
- And so their belief goes up and down.

00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:54.470
- So, are we just like three
really warm Aprils away

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.945
from people being like,
"Fine, let's fix this."

00:29:56.945 --> 00:29:58.320
(laughter)

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.070
- So, I'm sorry to return to this point.

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.970
The cynicism is killing
me. We are a nation

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:06.930
that the majority of us,
the majority of Republicans

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:09.470
believe that climate change is real.

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:11.710
The disconnect is not
the people of this nation

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:13.250
realizing that there's a problem.

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:16.762
The disconnect is, you know,
King used to always say,

00:30:16.762 --> 00:30:18.437
more eloquently than I could ever say it,

00:30:18.437 --> 00:30:20.410
"The problem today, what
we ought to repent for--"

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:21.243
- But wait, who said this again?

00:30:21.243 --> 00:30:22.076
- Martin Luther King.

00:30:22.076 --> 00:30:22.909
- Martin Luther King.

00:30:22.909 --> 00:30:24.727
- "What to repent for is
not the vitriolic words

00:30:24.727 --> 00:30:26.907
"and the violent actions
of the bad people,

00:30:26.907 --> 00:30:29.057
"it's the appalling silence and inaction

00:30:29.057 --> 00:30:30.220
"of the good people."

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:34.930
- And so, that's the problem.
(applause)

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:36.920
You poll Republicans, the
majority of them, as you said,

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:38.150
believe climate change is real.

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:42.070
Millennial Republicans are
so far more progressive

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:42.903
on these issues.
- Right.

00:30:42.903 --> 00:30:44.020
- On everything, in everything.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:44.853
- All issues.

00:30:44.853 --> 00:30:47.890
- And by the way, the
only major political party

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:52.330
on the planet Earth, every other nation,

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:54.260
their right party and
their left party, believe

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:55.200
in climate change.

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:57.720
The official, elected
Republican leadership

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:00.460
is the only one on the
planet Earth that does not

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:02.150
officially believe in climate change.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:04.144
- That's called American exceptionalism.

00:31:04.144 --> 00:31:06.540
(laughter)

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:10.251
- So should we put that
leadership on a different planet?

00:31:10.251 --> 00:31:11.084
- Yeah.

00:31:12.434 --> 00:31:14.180
- There ya go Neil.

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:16.950
- All right, let me give you
an example. This is simple.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:19.770
You know, I'm always a big
believer the power of the people

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.060
is greater than the people in power.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:23.870
But folks don't exercise their powers.

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:25.960
Alice Walker said the most
common way people give up their

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:29.460
power is not realizing they
have it in the first place.

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:31.500
And so the cynicism
that gets thrown around

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:33.790
is actually a toxic state of being,

00:31:33.790 --> 00:31:35.680
'cuz it's surrendering
your ability to make change

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:37.130
'cuz things can't be changed.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:39.580
This is something very simple.

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:42.543
If just Millennials alone,
Barack Obama said this

00:31:42.543 --> 00:31:44.920
in his speech to Howard students.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:47.160
Forget Republican or
Democrat, if just Millennial

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:48.840
generation, the biggest population bubble

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:50.750
coming up demographically right now,

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:54.080
if they just voted at the
same levels that X-Geners did,

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:56.510
40, 50% in midterm elections,

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:58.898
the entire Congress would change.

00:31:58.898 --> 00:32:01.700
- Thank God.
(applause)

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:04.100
- Obama said, this is not complicated.

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:05.097
He looked at the young people and said

00:32:05.097 --> 00:32:08.241
"You don't need to occupy
anything, just vote."

00:32:08.241 --> 00:32:13.241
And so this is not a problem
of knowing what is right to do,

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:15.447
and I fear--

00:32:15.447 --> 00:32:17.943
- How do we get that
message onto SnapChat?

00:32:19.632 --> 00:32:23.610
- So I get this, I get
that. And remembering,

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:27.040
that in the '60s, huge
protests, all the time,

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:29.440
in every major city, campus unrest.

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:32.480
It was a time where citizenry was trying

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:35.090
to take back the
government. And I get that.

00:32:35.090 --> 00:32:36.740
And you're getting some of that now.

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:39.700
But at the end of the day,
it comes down to policy.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:41.380
That's what it comes down to, doesn't it?

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:44.050
I mean, what policies
are in place, that we can

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:45.930
all agree to to solve these problems?

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:47.920
- But you can win fights.
Like I came in and I actually

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:50.990
took heat, even back here
in New Jersey, for arguing.

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:52.930
I said okay, I need to
figure out a deal to strike

00:32:52.930 --> 00:32:56.250
with Republicans, and I worked
with a lot of my colleagues

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.010
on this, and saying hey,
the problem is oil and gas

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:02.720
industry get all kinds of
tax credits for innovations.

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:05.830
But renewable energy,
which we are losing ground

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:08.237
to the Chinese and the
Germans, and their innovation

00:33:08.237 --> 00:33:11.170
and technology, the jobs of
the future, we're losing ground

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:14.610
because the tax credits for
wind and solar are one year.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:17.350
They're not predictable tax
credits that industry needs.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:19.070
And so we fought with an exchange.

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:21.680
We allowed the export of
oil, something we had banned,

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:24.680
in exchange for 7 years
of predictable tax credit.

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:26.010
Well as soon as Congress did that,

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:27.880
what do you think has
happened to the solar and wind

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:28.870
industry in the United States?

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:30.560
Boom. The investments are going up,

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:31.657
the innovation is going up.

00:33:31.657 --> 00:33:33.630
- That's the art of compromise.

00:33:33.630 --> 00:33:35.305
- We won that battle in Congress.

00:33:35.305 --> 00:33:37.580
It's not something that made
the front pages of newspapers,

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:39.400
but we're in there every day fighting.

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:41.970
And the thing that we
need from the public,

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:44.200
'cuz I've watched. This
has only been 100 days

00:33:44.200 --> 00:33:46.380
of the Trump Administration,
but people don't realize

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:49.110
the day Congress changed,
the new Congress came in,

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:50.600
one of the first things
the Republicans tried to do

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:53.210
in the House was to remove the watchdogs,

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:54.680
the ethics watchdogs.

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:58.290
And it was the public,
that so was outraged,

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:00.810
that they stopped them in their tracks

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:03.010
and they reversed courts.
I've seen that a number

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:06.190
of times since then, that
the public and the press,

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:08.620
exposing what's happening,
has helped move things back.

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:09.840
And you and I both know history.

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:12.580
When it comes to science,
the ability for the right

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:16.220
poets, the right inspiration
to prick the moral conscience

00:34:16.220 --> 00:34:17.280
and the urgency of people.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:19.580
Whether it's Kennedy talking
about going to the moon,

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:22.780
which has fueled science like crazy,

00:34:22.780 --> 00:34:26.390
or just simple Americans,
well-known people

00:34:26.390 --> 00:34:29.320
that have powerful platforms,
like Neil DeGrasse Tyson

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:32.020
bringing science--
(applause)

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:32.940
- Help us Neil DeGrasse
Tyson, you're our only hope.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:35.380
- Bringing science to the mainstream.

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:39.240
I mean, you are a guy
that is getting folk woke

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:41.990
on science issues, and I think
that that's really powerful.

00:34:41.990 --> 00:34:43.809
- I don't know, if woke and folks.

00:34:43.809 --> 00:34:45.900
(laughter)

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:48.460
Well no, what I'm trying to do is through

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:50.720
forums such as this and everything else,

00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:54.380
is just try to not tell
people what is true

00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:56.460
about the universe, but empower people

00:34:56.460 --> 00:34:58.060
to understand why.

00:34:58.060 --> 00:34:59.560
And in that way, they can take ownership

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:02.590
of that knowledge, without
even having to reference me.

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:03.423
Right?

00:35:03.423 --> 00:35:05.650
If all it was is, "This is
true 'cuz Tyson said so,"

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:06.970
then I failed as an educator.

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:09.367
You say, "This is true
'cuz A goes to B, and this

00:35:09.367 --> 00:35:11.350
"caused that, and that caused that,"

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:12.970
then you own that information.

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:15.900
And that then gets shared, and
I'm not even in that picture.

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:17.430
I don't have to be at that point.

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:19.087
And then everybody takes
command of their lives

00:35:19.087 --> 00:35:21.545
and of the country in which we live.

00:35:21.545 --> 00:35:24.571
(cheering)

00:35:24.571 --> 00:35:25.988
- What a concept.

00:35:27.033 --> 00:35:30.160
- It also helps a lot
of us not just wake up

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:32.700
everyday screaming 'cuz we know that

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.850
there's someone out
there that sounds sane.

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:38.633
- Okay, so one point.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:40.937
Carl Sagan was once asked--

00:35:42.326 --> 00:35:43.159
(cheering)

00:35:43.159 --> 00:35:45.070
Sagan, yeah.

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:47.909
- I think they were celebrating
that he asked a question.

00:35:47.909 --> 00:35:49.240
- Did you catch that the
senator early on said

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:51.520
billions and billions, did you hear that?

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:52.640
Yeah, yeah.

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:54.793
He actually said that, I heard that.

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:59.450
He was asked, with regard to superheros,

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:00.740
what was his favorite superhero.

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:04.210
And he's not a fan of
superheros. Because superheros,

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:09.210
as they're portrayed, it
gives us the excuse to not

00:36:09.620 --> 00:36:11.800
do anything about problems in the world,

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:14.160
because you're just
waiting around for someone

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:16.300
who has the power to
solve it, while you're

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:17.893
eating popcorn watching movies.

00:36:20.915 --> 00:36:24.873
And so to the Senator's point,
quoting, was it Alice Walker?

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:30.250
It's the power you don't
know you actually have

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:31.910
that is the failure.

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:32.743
- Yes.

00:36:32.743 --> 00:36:33.576
- I paraphrased.

00:36:33.576 --> 00:36:35.220
- Yes. That's one of the most common ways

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.991
we give up our power is
not realizing we have it,

00:36:37.991 --> 00:36:38.824
in the first place.

00:36:38.824 --> 00:36:40.010
- Not even knowing you
had already ceded it

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:41.340
to someone else who's using it.

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:42.173
- Yes.

00:36:42.173 --> 00:36:43.230
- Possibly against you.

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:46.260
- Most likely, if you
check out of the system,

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:47.397
that system is gonna work against you.

00:36:47.397 --> 00:36:49.740
- It's like when Time
Magazine said that we

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.585
were all Person of the Year.

00:36:51.585 --> 00:36:53.683
(laughter)

00:36:53.683 --> 00:36:55.780
- It did say that, I remember that.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:57.579
- Remember the mirror?

00:36:57.579 --> 00:36:59.210
- And I was lame, that was like the lamest

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:00.043
person of the year ever.

00:37:00.043 --> 00:37:01.380
- Or was it the best?

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:03.810
- No, it's, what's the generation where

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:05.773
there are no losers and
everyone is a winner?

00:37:05.773 --> 00:37:06.809
- Yes.
- Right.

00:37:06.809 --> 00:37:09.080
- I think they had the
editorial board in that moment.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:10.280
And yeah.

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:11.370
- Yeah.

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:12.640
- You think Carl Sagan secretly

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:13.946
liked "Green Lantern" though?

00:37:13.946 --> 00:37:16.196
(laughter)

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:19.350
- I'll check with his people on that.

00:37:19.350 --> 00:37:21.297
- Yeah, yeah, please do.

00:37:21.297 --> 00:37:23.350
- So, coming up in the next segment.

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:26.040
We're gonna explore,
and in fact, celebrate

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:28.010
the latest advances in medical research,

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:31.790
genetics, and health.
When StarTalk returns.

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:35.201
Live from the Count Basie
Theater, New Jersey!

00:37:35.201 --> 00:37:37.451
(applause)

00:37:42.417 --> 00:37:44.210
- [Audience Member] You're the man, Neil!

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:46.320
- The universe is the man.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:49.397
No, the universe is a gender
neutral human who we all love.

00:37:49.397 --> 00:37:51.090
(laughter)

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.620
- Red Bank , New Jersey!

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:54.223
Give it up for StarTalk!

00:37:54.223 --> 00:37:56.473
(applause)

00:37:59.659 --> 00:38:02.220
- Can I just point
something out about Eugene?

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:03.053
- Do your thing.

00:38:03.053 --> 00:38:05.660
- Eugene is one of the
proud representatives

00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:06.730
of one of the great traditions in America,

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:10.510
he is an immigrant from,
where are you from sir?

00:38:10.510 --> 00:38:11.343
- I forget.

00:38:11.343 --> 00:38:13.290
No, Russia.

00:38:13.290 --> 00:38:15.080
- You're from Russia.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:15.913
- Yeah.

00:38:15.913 --> 00:38:16.746
Yes.

00:38:16.746 --> 00:38:18.973
- Yeah. I'm a U.S. citizen,
so you can't get rid of me.

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:22.550
- That's right, so he's a
first generation immigrant.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:23.950
- He's a first generation immigrant.

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:26.010
You were born in another
country, you came here--

00:38:26.010 --> 00:38:28.385
- I can't be president, but that's fine.

00:38:28.385 --> 00:38:30.842
(laughter)

00:38:30.842 --> 00:38:33.600
- On that subject, I will add.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:38.600
I just did this homework,
that the decade by decade

00:38:38.620 --> 00:38:42.130
average of first generation
immigrants in the United States

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:46.810
since 1900 is about 10%.
So it's 1 in 10 Americans

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:49.440
were born somewhere
else, at any given time

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:50.670
across the century.

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:53.610
Now, let's ask another question.

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:57.070
What percentage of American
winners of the Nobel Prize

00:38:57.070 --> 00:39:00.630
in the sciences was a
first generation immigrant?

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:04.650
A third of all American
Nobel Prizes in chemistry,

00:39:04.650 --> 00:39:08.600
physics, and human
physiology, were foreign born.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:13.030
So they're three times as
represented in the science,

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:14.730
this highest prize of science,

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:16.680
than they are even in the population.

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:17.750
- Well I'll give you an example of this.

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:19.720
So people who come down
and lobby in Washington,

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:21.610
I love when people don't hire lobbyists

00:39:21.610 --> 00:39:22.590
but they come down themselves.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:24.370
I see lots of New Jersians come down,

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:26.610
and when I see college
professors from Princeton

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:29.610
to my University of Stanford
come, and they come to me

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:30.950
and they say, look, this is crazy.

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:32.890
We bring these folks
in, the brightest minds

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:35.530
to study at our universities
on student visas,

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:38.010
we use our resources, giving
them the best education

00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:39.970
on the planet Earth, and as
soon as that student visa

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:42.230
runs out, what is our
country now saying to them?

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:43.420
Get out of our nation.

00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:44.253
- Right.

00:39:44.253 --> 00:39:48.260
- And that's ridiculous,
when we have a country

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:50.630
that attracts its
greatness, and then sends it

00:39:50.630 --> 00:39:52.910
back out into the world.
(applause)

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:56.280
- I would like to point out,
on that particular point,

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:59.920
that President Obama
proposed in 2011 to staple

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:02.780
a Green card to every graduate degree

00:40:02.780 --> 00:40:04.530
in science earned by a foreign citizen.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:06.900
Just staple the Green card to the degree.

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:08.620
- And have them half illegal?

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:10.050
Let me tell you what's worse.

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:13.540
They have around Stanford, I
was told there was a billboard

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:16.417
that says, "If you can't get
your H-1B Visa" you know state,

00:40:16.417 --> 00:40:17.670
"come to Canada."

00:40:17.670 --> 00:40:21.100
And so other countries are
seeing what we used to do,

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.400
to accelerate ahead of the
rest of the planet Earth.

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:26.150
And one of those things
that they saw was that they

00:40:26.150 --> 00:40:28.470
had policies that tried
to attract the brightest

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:30.450
of the globe. And they're
saying, okay, America's

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:31.283
not gonna do that anymore?

00:40:31.283 --> 00:40:32.700
We wanna do that, 'cuz we wanna lead.

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:36.900
- Just in all fairness, Newt
Gingrich said that as well,

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:38.847
to staple the Green
card. Just so you know.

00:40:38.847 --> 00:40:41.023
I just wanna just be fair out there.

00:40:41.023 --> 00:40:42.390
- He said a lot of stuff.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:44.015
- One of his few sage observations.

00:40:44.015 --> 00:40:45.951
(laughter)

00:40:45.951 --> 00:40:49.570
- I am Canadian, and
all my Canadian friends

00:40:49.570 --> 00:40:52.447
now treat me like I have a illness.

00:40:52.447 --> 00:40:54.914
(laughter)

00:40:54.914 --> 00:40:55.813
- Is the illness being American?

00:40:55.813 --> 00:40:57.507
- Yeah, they're like, "How's it going?

00:40:57.507 --> 00:40:59.117
"Are you okay?"

00:41:00.736 --> 00:41:02.520
- So, two people on the
stage now are foreign born.

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:05.683
- Yep. And actually I'm
only on a Green card,

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:08.050
I'm not a citizen.
- Authorities, can you--

00:41:08.050 --> 00:41:11.380
- Yeah, hey. No I'm a new
mom, I have an anchor baby.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:12.265
I'm cool.

00:41:12.265 --> 00:41:13.098
Anchor baby.

00:41:13.098 --> 00:41:15.390
(laughter)

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:16.910
So let me ask you guys something.

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:20.633
I've got science advisors
here, I have a politician here,

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:26.790
clue us in how advice is

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:31.660
obtained, received, and enacted or not.

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:33.250
What is that dynamic here?

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:34.960
'Cuz I don't know.

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:37.570
- Well, first of all, I
had a great relationship

00:41:37.570 --> 00:41:39.712
with Democratic senators.

00:41:39.712 --> 00:41:41.648
- But not Republican senators?

00:41:41.648 --> 00:41:45.380
- A few Republican senators.
Many fewer. But I mean,

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:49.770
part of the way it works is
there's a lot of interaction

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:52.930
between the scientists in
government and Congress.

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:55.732
Scientists in government
testify, all the time--

00:41:55.732 --> 00:41:57.377
- In front of the subcommittees?

00:41:57.377 --> 00:41:59.040
- Or, in front of the
Senate and the House.

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:01.170
- You're on a committee. You're
on the science committee.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.370
- Yes, I'm on two
committees. One is called

00:42:03.370 --> 00:42:05.550
the commerce committee,
but the full name includes

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.610
commerce, technology, science,
a lot of those things.

00:42:07.610 --> 00:42:09.640
And I'm on the environment committee.

00:42:09.640 --> 00:42:10.850
Environment and public works.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:13.640
So I'm on two of the main
committees that deal with

00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:15.560
issues of science,
technology, and innovation.

00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:18.240
- And they hear a lot of
testimony, but they also

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:20.880
meet individually with
scientists and technologists

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:23.340
from, not just the White
House, but from the

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:25.910
Department of Energy, from the
National Science Foundation,

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:28.150
from NOAA, their staffs meet all the time.

00:42:28.150 --> 00:42:32.090
- So these are all the people who

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:33.970
the Senate approved.

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:34.929
- Yeah.

00:42:34.929 --> 00:42:38.680
- So they can just summon you at will.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:39.800
- Absolutely.

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:42.379
- And just, bitch slap you
when they feel like it.

00:42:42.379 --> 00:42:44.870
(laughter)

00:42:44.870 --> 00:42:46.177
- Well, you know, when I used to testify--

00:42:46.177 --> 00:42:48.457
- Is that a yes or a no? (laughter)

00:42:48.457 --> 00:42:52.610
- I will comment that on
days that I was testifying,

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:54.194
we used to call that pinata day.

00:42:54.194 --> 00:42:55.101
- Pinata day?

00:42:55.101 --> 00:42:57.090
(laughter)

00:42:57.090 --> 00:42:58.929
- They swing at you with
a big stick and they hope

00:42:58.929 --> 00:43:02.360
to break you open and
some candy will fall out.

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:03.950
- Did they ever hope you
would just help their kids

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:04.996
with their science homework?

00:43:04.996 --> 00:43:06.690
(laughter)

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:08.870
- 'Cuz I once testified in
front of this science committee

00:43:08.870 --> 00:43:12.300
in the Senate. And you
either weren't there,

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:13.730
or you weren't a Senator yet.

00:43:13.730 --> 00:43:17.107
- I would be there, sir,
I was not a senator yet.

00:43:17.107 --> 00:43:19.510
- But since I'm a
citizen, asked to testify,

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:21.620
I didn't feel like a pinata,
I felt that they were

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:23.450
just kind of gathering information.

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:25.900
And I was commenting on
the value of exploration,

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:29.060
specifically space
exploration into a universe

00:43:29.060 --> 00:43:31.860
that has unlimited resources,
especially the kinds

00:43:31.860 --> 00:43:34.490
of resources that on
Earth we fight wars over.

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:37.020
So I just thought I would
highlight this fact.

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:37.853
- Yeah.

00:43:37.853 --> 00:43:41.640
- And so, I was intrigued
because I didn't feel

00:43:41.640 --> 00:43:44.010
like I was making much of a difference.

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:46.360
And I was just kinda
going through motions,

00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:48.400
and then they were going through motions,

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:51.993
and I didn't feel their energy.

00:43:53.813 --> 00:43:54.670
So does this work?

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:56.730
When you give advice, are
they actually listening?

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.720
- Sometimes. Sometimes it
works, sometimes it doesn't.

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:02.590
- Cory, when they give
you advice, do you listen?

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:03.907
- Sometimes.

00:44:03.907 --> 00:44:06.157
(laughter)

00:44:07.930 --> 00:44:11.010
Look, there's a serious problem
with our politics at large,

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:14.670
but there is a massive area
on these science committees

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:17.830
in which I find bipartisan
space to work with.

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:20.880
And that's everything
from, I came to Washington

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:23.610
and I saw that the technology for drones,

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:26.520
that we were losing all
these companies 'cuz America,

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:30.270
our regulations, were
strangling that industry.

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:32.280
I told the head of the FAA,
if you were around during

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:33.990
Orville and Wilbur Wright,
we would've never gotten

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:34.910
off the ground.

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:37.040
And they were literally
going to other countries,

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:39.760
like France, and were
years and years ahead of us

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:42.850
in utilizing drones for mine
surveys, for technology,

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:44.040
and so I found partners there.

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:47.826
Nuclear energy, next
generation nuclear power is--

00:44:47.826 --> 00:44:48.950
- Thank you for pronouncing
nuclear correctly.

00:44:48.950 --> 00:44:49.991
Thank you very much.

00:44:49.991 --> 00:44:52.241
(laughter)

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:56.724
- Other nations are now
far more attractive to the

00:44:56.724 --> 00:45:00.610
nuclear scientists because
of our regulatory framework,

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:03.340
that doesn't look to the next
generation of nuclear energy,

00:45:03.340 --> 00:45:07.380
which is far safer, eats
the spent nuclear rods

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:12.000
from the current aversion,
and we were gonna be

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:13.887
losing out on our edge in that technology.

00:45:13.887 --> 00:45:15.640
- Here's something that I can't imagine--

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.150
- We need to loosen laws about nuclear...

00:45:18.150 --> 00:45:20.110
We need to make some loose nuclear energy?

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:21.700
- No.

00:45:21.700 --> 00:45:22.540
- That's exactly what he wants.

00:45:22.540 --> 00:45:24.990
- No, what we need to be doing as a nation

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:26.740
is we need to begin to have a government

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:29.107
that moves at the speed of
innovation, in other words--

00:45:29.107 --> 00:45:31.870
- That's the whole third
sector of this show.

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:33.564
- Right.
- We'll get back to that.

00:45:33.564 --> 00:45:34.397
- Okay.

00:45:34.397 --> 00:45:36.260
- What I wanna know is,
is one of the bipartisan

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:38.250
points of agreement health?

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:40.358
The National Institutes of Health.

00:45:40.358 --> 00:45:42.340
That's gotta be in there somewhere.

00:45:42.340 --> 00:45:43.920
- Well, look--

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:46.126
- Wow that took too long
to say yes. That's a no.

00:45:46.126 --> 00:45:48.490
(laughter)

00:45:48.490 --> 00:45:51.063
- Senator, that's a quick yes, all right.

00:45:52.141 --> 00:45:55.370
- So we are screwing
up, in the larger sense,

00:45:55.370 --> 00:45:57.590
in our investment in biotech.

00:45:57.590 --> 00:45:59.140
And coming from a biotech and innovation

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:01.950
in health space state, where--

00:46:01.950 --> 00:46:02.783
- New Jersey.

00:46:02.783 --> 00:46:04.940
- New Jersey is, we
are really screwing up,

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:07.030
in so many ways, the system.

00:46:07.030 --> 00:46:08.550
We're screwing up in not investing in it,

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:10.420
other nations are beginning to pull ahead

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:12.000
in their investments in this area.

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:14.370
We're screwing up in
the way the free market

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:16.883
is contorting and making
people's prescription drugs

00:46:16.883 --> 00:46:19.950
way too high priced. I
can go through the ways

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:22.460
that this space frustrates
the hell out of me.

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:24.710
But what bothered me most is just the fact

00:46:24.710 --> 00:46:27.530
that Alzeimer's, my
father died of Parkinson's

00:46:27.530 --> 00:46:30.780
just a few years ago, all
of this that is costing us,

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:33.720
these illnesses that are
costing us so much money,

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:37.180
we could be investing more in
the cures to these diseases.

00:46:37.180 --> 00:46:40.370
We could lead humanity out
of the darkness of the pain,

00:46:40.370 --> 00:46:42.700
like we look at other
diseases that we've conquered.

00:46:42.700 --> 00:46:45.650
But we're just not making
commitment as a society

00:46:45.650 --> 00:46:48.200
to put our resources,
or energy, or talent,

00:46:48.200 --> 00:46:49.033
or spirits to solve the problem.

00:46:49.033 --> 00:46:52.060
- John, it sounds like you
can combine that problem

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:55.610
with an analysis, and
throw in some economics,

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:58.450
and then you have an
argument that has no holds.

00:46:58.450 --> 00:46:59.670
That everyone just agrees with,

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:02.350
and you create the policy
and then you move on.

00:47:02.350 --> 00:47:04.543
Why isn't that happening?
- Well some of it is.

00:47:04.543 --> 00:47:06.500
- Everything you say makes complete sense.

00:47:06.500 --> 00:47:09.540
- Neil, some of it is happening.
In the Obama administration

00:47:09.540 --> 00:47:12.980
we started the Brain
Initiative, which is making

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:15.410
tremendous advances in
understanding how the brain works,

00:47:15.410 --> 00:47:18.110
which will help us ultimately
figure out how to cure

00:47:18.110 --> 00:47:19.110
or avoid Alzeimer's.

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.952
- Or how to make better
political decisions.

00:47:21.952 --> 00:47:24.202
(laughter)

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:28.269
- Does that still exist?
- No, it still exists.

00:47:28.269 --> 00:47:30.181
or has that just been replaced

00:47:30.181 --> 00:47:31.014
with like a bucket of bubbles.

00:47:31.014 --> 00:47:32.060
- It still exists, maybe
'cuz they haven't found

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:32.910
out about it yet.

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:34.783
- Wait so Jo,

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:37.870
can you think of biomedical advances

00:47:37.870 --> 00:47:40.800
that more people need to know about?

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:43.380
- Well, one of the big
initiatives that I worked on,

00:47:43.380 --> 00:47:45.630
in fact, it was my first
initiative in the White House,

00:47:45.630 --> 00:47:47.200
was precision medicine.

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:49.580
Which is the idea of using big data

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:53.680
about big populations of
people to tailor medicine,

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:56.520
treatment, and prevention
to the individual.

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:59.120
So you can sort of parse
people into lots of different

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:02.740
groups, and based on,
whether it's their zip codes

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:06.440
or their genome, or their
microbiome, something like that,

00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:08.680
you can then make predictions
about their health.

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:11.390
And this is really the future of medicine.

00:48:11.390 --> 00:48:13.550
But I had kind of an
interesting experience.

00:48:13.550 --> 00:48:16.680
This was my first memo to the president.

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:19.910
We, John and I, wrote this
memo, and then we were invited

00:48:19.910 --> 00:48:22.470
a few days later, very
quickly, to the Oval Office

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:23.470
to meet with the president.

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:25.439
- You got to write memos to the president?

00:48:25.439 --> 00:48:26.272
- Yeah.

00:48:26.272 --> 00:48:27.105
- Can we tell which
ones will commit crimes

00:48:27.105 --> 00:48:28.430
and arrest them beforehand?

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:29.410
(laughter)

00:48:29.410 --> 00:48:31.870
- I saw that movie, yeah. Okay.

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:34.540
- So we had this great
meeting, and the president

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:36.900
clearly had gotten precision medicine,

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:38.900
so I walked out feeling pretty good.

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:42.097
I said, "John, he got it.
And this is pretty cool."

00:48:42.097 --> 00:48:44.320
And the next week I was sort of perusing

00:48:44.320 --> 00:48:48.720
some old legislation, and I
came across 2006 legislation

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:52.311
written by Senator Obama
on precision medicine.

00:48:52.311 --> 00:48:53.860
(laughter)

00:48:53.860 --> 00:48:57.040
Crushed. I was simply crushed.

00:48:57.040 --> 00:49:00.310
But that I think, is an
example of a bipartisan issue,

00:49:00.310 --> 00:49:02.500
because when we rolled
out precision medicine,

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:05.370
we had as many Republicans as Democrats

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:09.560
at the event. And just in
December, the 21st century

00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:12.210
Cures Bill, which supports
precision medicine

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:15.010
and the Brain Initiative,
and several other things,

00:49:15.010 --> 00:49:17.450
passed the Senate 95 to 5.

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:19.670
Thank you very much.
(applause)

00:49:19.670 --> 00:49:21.463
And that was certainly bipartisan.

00:49:22.460 --> 00:49:24.195
- He was in the five that didn't--

00:49:24.195 --> 00:49:25.840
(laughter)

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:27.450
- Yeah, let's hear a
thank you sarcastic 'cuz

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:28.483
he voted against it.

00:49:29.849 --> 00:49:33.210
- Yeah, thank you very
much. So it would seem

00:49:33.210 --> 00:49:34.810
to me that health would be the most

00:49:34.810 --> 00:49:36.600
bipartisan thing going.
- I agree.

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:40.520
- But then, I'm surprised
to see a proposal to reduce

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:42.870
the funding to the National
Institutes of Health,

00:49:42.870 --> 00:49:44.240
so I don't understand that.

00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:46.020
- By almost 6 billion dollars.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:48.260
- Yeah, so Cory, what's up with that?

00:49:48.260 --> 00:49:49.190
- It is a--

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:51.538
(laughter)

00:49:51.538 --> 00:49:52.856
- What's up with that?

00:49:52.856 --> 00:49:54.160
(laughter)

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:56.030
- I've literally been the scrum

00:49:56.030 --> 00:49:57.240
during these large budget deals,

00:49:57.240 --> 00:50:00.480
where exacerbatingly, you're
fighting to try to say

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:04.660
how can we be funding x,
y, or z, like these broken

00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:06.950
programs that don't do
anything, and we're not funding

00:50:06.950 --> 00:50:09.300
something as obvious
as this, that frankly,

00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:12.620
I work in a body, that
we're all getting old.

00:50:12.620 --> 00:50:15.930
It's kinda one of the more
thoughtful senior bodies

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:18.320
where a lot of these
diseases are gonna be visited

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:20.608
upon us. And so, I don't understand.

00:50:20.608 --> 00:50:23.090
Forget about if you don't
care about this country,

00:50:23.090 --> 00:50:25.650
and you're not as much of a
patriot as we all should be,

00:50:25.650 --> 00:50:28.240
but think about your family,
think about yourself.

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:29.760
Why aren't we making
more of an investment?

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:31.600
And even worse, again, I
keep repeating this over

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:34.070
and over again, but we've heard all about

00:50:34.070 --> 00:50:36.670
this president promising
that we're gonna win bigly,

00:50:38.595 --> 00:50:42.420
but the reality is, our
competitors are making

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:45.060
massive investments in terms
of percentage of their GDP

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:47.610
investing in these things,
they're overtaking us.

00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:49.280
They're gonna catch us and overtake us.

00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:51.930
And so, I just look at
what China is doing,

00:50:51.930 --> 00:50:55.400
and what Germany is doing,
and what Russia is even doing

00:50:55.400 --> 00:50:56.550
in terms of what they're investing in--

00:50:56.550 --> 00:50:58.977
- Even Russia?
(laughter)

00:50:58.977 --> 00:51:02.180
- But Cory, I'm tired of something.

00:51:02.180 --> 00:51:03.013
I'm angry.

00:51:03.013 --> 00:51:06.498
- Yes, okay. Bring it.
Let your inner Jersey out.

00:51:06.498 --> 00:51:08.393
(laughter)

00:51:08.393 --> 00:51:09.226
- Hold me back.

00:51:11.780 --> 00:51:14.720
So the day I realized,

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:17.890
and this is a pretty, I'm
gonna call it upsetting,

00:51:17.890 --> 00:51:19.740
but disturbing day for me.

00:51:19.740 --> 00:51:24.740
When I look back at America's
presence in the Space Race,

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:30.550
okay, from 1957 onwards,
the launch of Sputnik,

00:51:30.550 --> 00:51:31.950
until we landed on the moon.

00:51:33.950 --> 00:51:38.240
Essentially every decision
we made, to go into space,

00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:42.297
and what to do there, was
reactive to what Russia

00:51:42.297 --> 00:51:44.880
and the Soviet Union had already done

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:48.950
or was already planning.
Every single move.

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:50.910
They put in a first satellite,
we had a first satellite.

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:53.120
They put up a dog, we put up a chimp.

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:55.530
They put up a human,
then we put up a human.

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:59.600
And we are reacting, at
every time, at every turn.

00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:03.730
And I wonder, can a
democracy be proactive?

00:52:03.730 --> 00:52:06.540
Or do we have to wait around
until we feel threatened,

00:52:06.540 --> 00:52:08.850
and only then do the pistons align for us

00:52:08.850 --> 00:52:11.050
to act the way we should.

00:52:11.050 --> 00:52:13.300
(applause)

00:52:17.292 --> 00:52:19.062
- We're live at the Count Basie Theater!

00:52:19.062 --> 00:52:20.730
(applause)

00:52:20.730 --> 00:52:23.860
We're talking about the
marriage of science and policy.

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:25.975
And I've got a great panel up here,

00:52:25.975 --> 00:52:30.090
what we're tryna do is
make America smart again.

00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:34.725
Trying to find out how policy
and science come together

00:52:34.725 --> 00:52:38.440
to affect change, for the
greater good of us all.

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:40.330
And Eugene, we're here for you.

00:52:40.330 --> 00:52:43.328
- Yes, thank you.

00:52:43.328 --> 00:52:45.750
- It's still part of the
Eugene Mirman Comedy Festival.

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:47.200
Can we set the stage here?

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:48.332
- Yes, let's do it.

00:52:48.332 --> 00:52:51.384
With Baratunde, everybody.

00:52:51.384 --> 00:52:53.846
(applause)

00:52:53.846 --> 00:52:55.245
And Ophira Eisenburg.

00:52:55.245 --> 00:52:57.550
(applause)

00:52:57.550 --> 00:53:00.120
- And I've got John
Haldren and Jo Hendelsman,

00:53:00.120 --> 00:53:02.100
both who worked at Obama's

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:05.480
Office of Science and Technology Policy.

00:53:05.480 --> 00:53:08.750
And very special guest, who
wandered off the streets

00:53:08.750 --> 00:53:12.528
onto stage, we have New
Jersey senator Cory Booker.

00:53:12.528 --> 00:53:14.070
(applause)

00:53:14.070 --> 00:53:17.640
- Hey Neil, I hate to say
anything defending the

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:20.300
U.S Space Program, but the Russians never

00:53:20.300 --> 00:53:23.310
put up the Hubble, we put
up the Hubble without any--

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:24.820
- Oh, snap!

00:53:24.820 --> 00:53:25.653
- Son, what?

00:53:25.653 --> 00:53:27.520
- Precedence from the Russians.

00:53:27.520 --> 00:53:28.573
- 20 years later.

00:53:29.540 --> 00:53:32.402
- No, but you're talking
about our reactive stance

00:53:32.402 --> 00:53:34.750
through the '60s. We've
done a lot of things

00:53:34.750 --> 00:53:35.800
ahead of the Russians--

00:53:35.800 --> 00:53:38.530
- Right, Hubble was peanuts compared

00:53:38.530 --> 00:53:41.123
with putting people in
space, that's all I'm saying.

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:45.423
- But scientifically not peanuts, at all.

00:53:45.423 --> 00:53:47.260
- Of course, I got that.
But most of the budget NASA

00:53:47.260 --> 00:53:50.320
was always not science, so.

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:51.882
- Yeah, we tried to fix that.

00:53:51.882 --> 00:53:53.814
(laughter)

00:53:53.814 --> 00:53:55.970
- Now, let me get back to
biomedical advances here.

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:59.260
- Do you think though,
that if ISIS tried to cure

00:53:59.260 --> 00:54:00.900
Parkinson's, we would then--

00:54:00.900 --> 00:54:01.733
- Get on it.

00:54:01.733 --> 00:54:03.254
(laughter)

00:54:03.254 --> 00:54:06.670
- Like if they kind of shifted,
maybe their priorities,

00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:08.070
we could get in a real race.

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:12.000
- So Jo, what more advances
are coming down the pipe?

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:12.940
What can we look for?

00:54:12.940 --> 00:54:15.930
- Well, I think one of the
big areas that I'm excited

00:54:15.930 --> 00:54:18.170
about is microbiology,
because we're starting

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:20.570
to understand that not
only the human body,

00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:23.820
but all ecosystems on
Earth are driven in part

00:54:23.820 --> 00:54:26.300
by their microbiomes, which
means their collection

00:54:26.300 --> 00:54:27.904
of microorganisms.

00:54:27.904 --> 00:54:29.390
- The microorganisms that
live on you and in you.

00:54:29.390 --> 00:54:30.250
- Yeah. Exactly.

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:32.540
- Like mostly, probably,
in your intestines.

00:54:32.540 --> 00:54:34.040
- Right, that's where a lot of them are.

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:37.796
But also, the skin, the ears,
every orifice of the body.

00:54:37.796 --> 00:54:38.672
- And it turns out--

00:54:38.672 --> 00:54:39.505
- This is nasty.

00:54:39.505 --> 00:54:40.367
- No, no.

00:54:40.367 --> 00:54:43.180
- I'm thinking, you know
you told anyone under 30

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:46.450
that everyone over 50,
at one time, would walk

00:54:46.450 --> 00:54:48.653
into something called a phone booth,

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:53.690
and take the receiver and
put this side to their ear,

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:57.210
and this side to their
mouth, where 100 other people

00:54:57.210 --> 00:54:58.490
have done it that day.

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:02.003
So now you have shared
earwax and mouth spill.

00:55:03.223 --> 00:55:04.292
- But that illustrates--

00:55:04.292 --> 00:55:05.250
- There's no but to that!

00:55:05.250 --> 00:55:06.407
That's just nasty.

00:55:06.407 --> 00:55:08.167
(laughter)

00:55:08.167 --> 00:55:09.318
- All right, they're still alive though.

00:55:09.318 --> 00:55:10.280
- Maybe that's why the
old generation doesn't

00:55:10.280 --> 00:55:12.270
have all these allergies and disease.

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:16.100
We're like steeped in germs.

00:55:16.100 --> 00:55:17.447
- Well, and that's right.

00:55:17.447 --> 00:55:21.650
And it points to exactly the
issue, that most microbes

00:55:21.650 --> 00:55:24.360
are not germs, they're not harmful to us.

00:55:24.360 --> 00:55:27.000
There are only about 80 species of germs,

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:29.130
and there are thousands, if not millions,

00:55:29.130 --> 00:55:30.780
of other species of microbes.

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:33.490
And so, we don't think
of them as the good guys.

00:55:33.490 --> 00:55:35.760
But in fact, they're keeping us healthy,

00:55:35.760 --> 00:55:38.500
controlling our behavior,
controlling our vulnerability

00:55:38.500 --> 00:55:39.990
to disease all the time.

00:55:39.990 --> 00:55:42.840
- But even, I've read a lot
of the research on this,

00:55:42.840 --> 00:55:45.760
I mean, even things like
depression, and a lot of things

00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:48.060
that we've been thinking
have other actives

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:51.420
actually our gut microbiome
is affecting so much

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:54.110
of our wellbeing, how we're dealing--

00:55:54.110 --> 00:55:56.430
- That's a whole frontier now.
- It is an amazing frontier.

00:55:56.430 --> 00:55:58.050
- Wait, that it has to do with depression?

00:55:58.050 --> 00:55:59.700
- Yes, talk, talk.

00:55:59.700 --> 00:56:01.150
- Tell me. I am very curious.

00:56:04.172 --> 00:56:05.640
- You tell me the microbes in my body

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:06.755
are affecting the--

00:56:06.755 --> 00:56:09.255
(Neil laughs)

00:56:10.687 --> 00:56:12.140
I gotta say this 'cuz it was so cool.

00:56:12.140 --> 00:56:15.050
She was saying that there's
some, correct me if I'm wrong,

00:56:15.050 --> 00:56:16.290
I know I'll get some of this wrong.

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:18.720
She was saying that
there are microbes in you

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:21.710
that actually like
chocolate, and communicate

00:56:21.710 --> 00:56:24.990
this fact to your eating desires.

00:56:24.990 --> 00:56:26.790
And you say, "Gee, I want some chocolate."

00:56:26.790 --> 00:56:29.740
When in fact, it's your
microbiome that's asking for it.

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:30.946
- That's right.

00:56:30.946 --> 00:56:31.969
- That's true!

00:56:31.969 --> 00:56:34.460
- We're driven by our
bacteria, absolutely.

00:56:34.460 --> 00:56:36.130
- But I hate to tell you
this, at least everything

00:56:36.130 --> 00:56:39.370
that I'm reading is,
there's good gut bacteria,

00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:42.760
bad gut bacteria, and the bad
gut bacteria really breeds

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:45.770
off of empty carbohydrates
and things like that.

00:56:45.770 --> 00:56:48.090
But if you really wanna
breed better gut bacteria,

00:56:48.090 --> 00:56:50.358
you need to eat more
fiber, more vegetables,

00:56:50.358 --> 00:56:51.999
more plant-based diet.

00:56:51.999 --> 00:56:52.832
(applause)

00:56:52.832 --> 00:56:54.611
- Five people are
pro-fiber in this audience.

00:56:54.611 --> 00:56:57.223
- Clapping for carrots.

00:56:58.160 --> 00:57:01.520
- So tell me about this
gene editing tool, CRISPR,

00:57:01.520 --> 00:57:03.190
that I've heard. That's an acronym right?

00:57:03.190 --> 00:57:04.023
- Yep.

00:57:04.023 --> 00:57:07.230
- 'Cuz this sounds like it's
the future of all biology.

00:57:07.230 --> 00:57:09.700
- Well, I think it's very
important, because it lets us

00:57:09.700 --> 00:57:13.550
make very, very precise changes
in genes or around genes.

00:57:13.550 --> 00:57:15.275
- This is a tool in the laboratory?

00:57:15.275 --> 00:57:16.108
- That's right.

00:57:16.108 --> 00:57:17.193
- That could never go wrong.

00:57:17.193 --> 00:57:20.143
(laughter)

00:57:20.143 --> 00:57:21.893
It's like a photoshop for genetics.

00:57:23.090 --> 00:57:24.320
What could you do wrong with that?

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:25.453
- What could go wrong?

00:57:27.183 --> 00:57:28.180
- Can they grow hair?

00:57:28.180 --> 00:57:31.700
- So, is a biologist concerned
about the ethics of that?

00:57:31.700 --> 00:57:35.410
Making new life, or altering
life to your own whims?

00:57:35.410 --> 00:57:37.860
- Yeah, well I think that was a big issue

00:57:37.860 --> 00:57:40.020
when John and I were in the White House,

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:42.300
was trying to figure
out, what are the limits

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:43.920
to what we're comfortable with?

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:47.470
And one that was clear,
and the president said this

00:57:47.470 --> 00:57:51.590
in his policy, was that we're
not gonna edit the germline,

00:57:51.590 --> 00:57:54.210
which means the embryos that are forming.

00:57:54.210 --> 00:57:56.900
So we're not gonna
create heritable changes

00:57:56.900 --> 00:57:59.320
in people in the test tube.

00:57:59.320 --> 00:58:02.050
- Heritable would mean
the ability to transfer

00:58:02.050 --> 00:58:03.183
that from one generation to the next.

00:58:03.183 --> 00:58:04.058
- That's right.

00:58:04.058 --> 00:58:04.891
- Right.

00:58:04.891 --> 00:58:07.490
- And so we're thinking
more in terms of what used

00:58:07.490 --> 00:58:10.620
to be called gene therapy,
where regular tissue,

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:15.280
not your sexual tissue, but
your skin, or your heart,

00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:17.310
or your lungs, would be modified.

00:58:17.310 --> 00:58:20.160
So it would only have an
effect in your lifetime.

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:22.220
But that hasn't stopped
the Chinese from doing

00:58:22.220 --> 00:58:24.940
exactly the experiments
we decided not to do,

00:58:24.940 --> 00:58:28.740
and affecting embryos,
and having gene changes

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:29.894
that will be passed on.

00:58:29.894 --> 00:58:31.294
- Do we have super soldiers?

00:58:32.150 --> 00:58:33.460
- I saw that movie.

00:58:33.460 --> 00:58:35.872
- Yeah, it sounds like it might be real.

00:58:35.872 --> 00:58:37.040
(laughter)

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:39.630
- Or at least people that
live off of chocolate only.

00:58:39.630 --> 00:58:41.210
That'd be cool.

00:58:41.210 --> 00:58:46.210
- I mean, it seems to me if
you can modify the individual,

00:58:46.440 --> 00:58:50.070
you can, you know we joke about this,

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:52.930
and I'm not even a fan of
it, but people are imagining

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:54.760
if you're gonna live on
Mars, just genetically

00:58:54.760 --> 00:58:56.720
modify you so that
everything that's different

00:58:56.720 --> 00:58:59.870
about Mars is okay for your
genetically modified body.

00:58:59.870 --> 00:59:02.440
And that way you don't have
to live in a hab module.

00:59:02.440 --> 00:59:06.010
That's an extreme case,
but clearly you could use

00:59:06.010 --> 00:59:09.200
this to cure us of our
traditional diseases.

00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:11.520
- Right. And so the human body has evolved

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:14.430
over many millennia to
be what it is today,

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:16.650
with a few mistakes, certainly.

00:59:16.650 --> 00:59:18.880
But we haven't evolved to be on Mars.

00:59:18.880 --> 00:59:21.045
So I don't think we're just
gonna make a few tweaks--

00:59:21.045 --> 00:59:22.290
- I just put that out there,

00:59:22.290 --> 00:59:24.085
'cuz people occasionally talk about it.

00:59:24.085 --> 00:59:24.918
- Yeah.

00:59:24.918 --> 00:59:27.600
- But is this real, and is
NIH funding this research?

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:29.423
And does Congress know about this?

00:59:29.423 --> 00:59:30.600
And are they behind it?

00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:32.490
- Are you learning about it now, here?

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:34.450
- Yeah, yeah he's taking notes.

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:36.004
- And have you watched WestWorld?

00:59:36.004 --> 00:59:38.466
(laughter)

00:59:38.466 --> 00:59:41.018
- And can we delete the Republican gene?

00:59:41.018 --> 00:59:44.351
(laughter and applause)

00:59:45.390 --> 00:59:46.840
- Edit, Ophira, edit.

00:59:46.840 --> 00:59:51.653
- Sorry, sorry. I know that's
mind control but I'm for it.

00:59:55.035 --> 00:59:58.090
- So, is there an awareness of the value

00:59:58.090 --> 00:59:59.313
of that power?

01:00:01.841 --> 01:00:03.449
The good value of that power.

01:00:03.449 --> 01:00:06.143
- Incredibly so. It's not to
where I would want it to be,

01:00:06.143 --> 01:00:09.350
I would like us to get
back to being a science,

01:00:09.350 --> 01:00:13.190
technology, innovation
leading nation, and that's

01:00:13.190 --> 01:00:15.390
my frustration. Is the
excitement that I get

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:17.180
when I hear a scientist
like this talk about

01:00:17.180 --> 01:00:21.050
what is possible, I wish
we could somehow, sort of,

01:00:21.050 --> 01:00:23.020
expand the moral
imagination of this country

01:00:23.020 --> 01:00:25.680
about what we are capable
about in terms of leading

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:29.380
the human race into a
safer, into a stronger,

01:00:29.380 --> 01:00:32.420
into a more prosperous
world for all of us.

01:00:32.420 --> 01:00:34.410
And that's the challenge
we have right now.

01:00:34.410 --> 01:00:36.410
I get back to this idea
of what I think you play

01:00:36.410 --> 01:00:39.270
a good role in, and we all
have to accept responsibility

01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:43.600
in doing, is we can't
expect the world to change,

01:00:43.600 --> 01:00:45.320
unless we're willing to change.

01:00:45.320 --> 01:00:47.730
And be a part of that
change, and lead that change.

01:00:47.730 --> 01:00:50.810
And so, we all should be
excited about science,

01:00:50.810 --> 01:00:53.420
excited about innovation, the
more we get excited about it,

01:00:53.420 --> 01:00:55.173
the more that will ripple
out. The more we demand our

01:00:55.173 --> 01:00:58.000
elected leaders are,
the more likely they are

01:00:58.000 --> 01:00:59.510
to respond to our demands.

01:00:59.510 --> 01:01:03.530
- And so, what do you see are the barriers

01:01:03.530 --> 01:01:08.330
between that goal and sort
of, making America smart?

01:01:08.330 --> 01:01:13.330
In a way that we become wise,

01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:15.530
wise shepherds of our future.

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:17.160
- Well, look, I wanna be very blunt.

01:01:17.160 --> 01:01:19.920
We are gonna have some
very tough, fighting years

01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:20.780
ahead of us.

01:01:20.780 --> 01:01:24.570
We've got three plus
years now, of a president,

01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:27.500
who has made it clear
on many of these issues,

01:01:27.500 --> 01:01:30.370
that he is contrary. You
know, that the Chinese

01:01:30.370 --> 01:01:33.150
made up global warming.
You saw what the values

01:01:33.150 --> 01:01:34.800
with his budget he put forward.

01:01:34.800 --> 01:01:37.500
And so much of what I'm doing
in Washington right now,

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:39.500
still looking for
partnerships across the aisle

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:41.840
to get things done, but
I'm preparing to fight

01:01:41.840 --> 01:01:45.130
a president that I think wants
to take our country backwards

01:01:45.130 --> 01:01:46.710
in terms of science, innovation--

01:01:46.710 --> 01:01:50.980
- Yeah, but I don't beat
politicians over the head.

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:51.813
You know why?

01:01:51.813 --> 01:01:55.370
Because they're elected
by an electorate, right?

01:01:55.370 --> 01:01:56.203
- Right.

01:01:56.203 --> 01:01:57.756
- So, you can beat 'em on the head,

01:01:57.756 --> 01:02:00.330
and even get rid of them,
but then there's the matter

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:02.935
of the electorate that voted
for them in the first place.

01:02:02.935 --> 01:02:03.768
- Right.

01:02:03.768 --> 01:02:06.040
- So your gripe is not
actually with the president,

01:02:06.040 --> 01:02:08.070
your gripe is with the 60
million people who voted for him.

01:02:08.070 --> 01:02:09.530
- No no, in fact,
(applause)

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:10.363
hold on.

01:02:11.633 --> 01:02:15.130
I don't think we get anywhere as a country

01:02:15.130 --> 01:02:18.730
when we are in the course
of demonizing each other,

01:02:18.730 --> 01:02:19.710
I think what we need to do--

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:21.613
- I see this as a matter of education.

01:02:21.613 --> 01:02:22.974
- Well, yes.

01:02:22.974 --> 01:02:25.327
- If you're actually saying,
this policy will harm

01:02:25.327 --> 01:02:27.260
these people and they don't know it,

01:02:27.260 --> 01:02:29.030
then somebody's gotta educate them.

01:02:29.030 --> 01:02:30.620
- Right. And I'll give
you two quick examples.

01:02:30.620 --> 01:02:33.720
One is, this is why the
Science March is so important.

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:35.127
Because when you saw--

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:38.136
- 8 people are goin' to the Science March.

01:02:38.136 --> 01:02:38.969
(laughter)

01:02:38.969 --> 01:02:41.170
- I was down in Washington
for the Women's March,

01:02:41.170 --> 01:02:42.983
and people didn't march around saying,

01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:47.210
it wasn't people with signs
like beat Republicans.

01:02:47.210 --> 01:02:49.530
In fact, I bumped into
women that were Republicans

01:02:49.530 --> 01:02:52.630
there that were against
a lot of policy issues.

01:02:52.630 --> 01:02:53.463
But a lot of this is just--

01:02:53.463 --> 01:02:55.547
- Right, I saw no
anti-republican signs, at all.

01:02:55.547 --> 01:02:56.590
- No, not one.

01:02:56.590 --> 01:02:58.070
- And there were a lot
of innovative signs.

01:02:58.070 --> 01:03:00.070
- The March on Washington,
you had people like

01:03:00.070 --> 01:03:02.730
Strom Thurmand, literally
the longest fillibuster

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:06.530
in the Senate, is a racist
rant by a man trying to block

01:03:06.530 --> 01:03:07.760
the Civil Rights legislation.

01:03:07.760 --> 01:03:10.370
But the March on Washington,
listen to the speakers.

01:03:10.370 --> 01:03:13.000
John Lewis, Martin Luther
King, they weren't speaking

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:15.450
against those folks, they were calling

01:03:15.450 --> 01:03:17.450
to the moral imagination of this country.

01:03:17.450 --> 01:03:22.450
And what my frustration is,
is often we are not engaged.

01:03:22.730 --> 01:03:26.180
We luxuriate in this incredible nation,

01:03:26.180 --> 01:03:27.903
we have the four most
powerful words you can say

01:03:27.903 --> 01:03:30.550
as a human being. In fact,
only 4 and a half percent

01:03:30.550 --> 01:03:34.330
of humanity can say, "I am an American."

01:03:34.330 --> 01:03:37.740
And that comes because of
the labors and sacrifices

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:39.820
and struggles of generations before.

01:03:39.820 --> 01:03:43.600
And this generation, we see
what happens when we disconnect.

01:03:43.600 --> 01:03:45.630
We see what's happening
in Washington as a result

01:03:45.630 --> 01:03:49.360
of people not voting. I
saw this one pie graph,

01:03:49.360 --> 01:03:52.600
you know, 50, 60 million people
voting for Hillary Clinton.

01:03:52.600 --> 01:03:55.300
57 for Donald Trump,
million for Donald Trump.

01:03:55.300 --> 01:03:57.217
And 74 million other
people like, "Oh my god,

01:03:57.217 --> 01:03:59.083
"look what just happened." You know?

01:03:59.083 --> 01:04:02.132
(laughter)

01:04:02.132 --> 01:04:05.780
And so, I'll give a very
real example of the EPA

01:04:05.780 --> 01:04:07.320
and what's happening right now.

01:04:07.320 --> 01:04:09.300
This isn't because of Donald
Trump. This was happening

01:04:09.300 --> 01:04:11.600
under a great president that
wished he could change it.

01:04:11.600 --> 01:04:14.590
We, in our nation right
now, where Ronald Regan

01:04:14.590 --> 01:04:17.880
reauthorized and Mitch
McConnell voted for,

01:04:17.880 --> 01:04:21.760
a simple solution to
clean up Superfund sites.

01:04:21.760 --> 01:04:24.566
These are corporations that
create the most toxic spots

01:04:24.566 --> 01:04:28.820
in all of America. There's
a Superfund in every state,

01:04:28.820 --> 01:04:31.600
unfortunately, New Jersey
has the most of them.

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:33.220
Now, that has--

01:04:33.220 --> 01:04:34.172
- Lucky you!

01:04:34.172 --> 01:04:35.630
(laughter)

01:04:35.630 --> 01:04:39.140
- That has lapsed, that
funding has lapsed.

01:04:39.140 --> 01:04:41.970
Because this Congress now,
suddenly, not like Regan,

01:04:41.970 --> 01:04:44.120
not like the old Mitch
McConnell, decides not

01:04:44.120 --> 01:04:45.850
to reauthorize the cleanup for that.

01:04:45.850 --> 01:04:48.270
So there's all of these
so called orphan sites,

01:04:48.270 --> 01:04:50.570
there's no corporation anymore
to go after to clean them up.

01:04:50.570 --> 01:04:52.560
But now, we have something called data.

01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:54.410
When I was mayor, I
learned this real quick.

01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:55.870
A lot of people coming
to me, a lot of emotion,

01:04:55.870 --> 01:04:58.727
and I said, "Look, in God
we trust, but everybody else

01:04:58.727 --> 01:05:00.840
"bring me data." You're not a deity.

01:05:00.840 --> 01:05:02.680
Show me the numbers. Well,
now we have longitudinal

01:05:02.680 --> 01:05:04.650
data, from Princeton University,

01:05:04.650 --> 01:05:07.080
about what are the long
term effects of living

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:10.490
around a Superfund site?
And we now know that if you

01:05:10.490 --> 01:05:13.060
have a child around a
Superfund site, there's

01:05:13.060 --> 01:05:15.950
about a 20% more likely
of increase in autism,

01:05:15.950 --> 01:05:18.850
20% more likely of
increase in birth defects.

01:05:18.850 --> 01:05:21.040
So talk about a threat to our children.

01:05:21.040 --> 01:05:23.980
This isn't the Russians or ISIS coming,

01:05:23.980 --> 01:05:26.710
this is problems we have
right here, in our country,

01:05:26.710 --> 01:05:29.470
that the only things allowing
these to proliferate,

01:05:29.470 --> 01:05:32.010
I have two Superfund sites in Newark

01:05:32.010 --> 01:05:33.890
that are close to where I live.

01:05:33.890 --> 01:05:36.040
But the only thing stopping
us from doing something

01:05:36.040 --> 01:05:37.710
is decisions being made in Congress.

01:05:37.710 --> 01:05:39.330
But most of us don't even know that fact.

01:05:39.330 --> 01:05:41.870
- But we also, we have the gene editing.

01:05:41.870 --> 01:05:43.900
So we can just get that
deployed there first.

01:05:43.900 --> 01:05:46.330
- I guess what I'm saying is that

01:05:46.330 --> 01:05:49.160
this is the greatest
country on the planet Earth,

01:05:49.160 --> 01:05:50.980
I don't care what Donald
Trump says, that we need

01:05:50.980 --> 01:05:53.520
to make it great again,
we are an amazing country,

01:05:53.520 --> 01:05:56.920
with reservoirs of love,
and goodness, and kindness.

01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:59.110
But something is missing.
And it was missing

01:05:59.110 --> 01:06:02.160
in the 1960's too. It took geniuses.

01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:04.190
I remember Martin Luther
King, if you know the history

01:06:04.190 --> 01:06:06.760
of Taylor Branch. He comes
out of Birmingham Jail

01:06:06.760 --> 01:06:08.500
after writing one of the greatest pieces

01:06:08.500 --> 01:06:10.860
of American literature, "Letters
from a Birmingham Jail,"

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:15.200
but he was failing. Two young
people, with an imagination,

01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:17.757
Dorothy Cotton and James
Bevel, came up to him and said,

01:06:17.757 --> 01:06:21.050
"Hey you're failing here. Let
us try something different."

01:06:21.050 --> 01:06:22.610
And the thing they did different was

01:06:22.610 --> 01:06:25.860
to organize other young
people, ages 8 to 18,

01:06:25.860 --> 01:06:28.930
to march against Bull Connor.
To create the spectacle

01:06:28.930 --> 01:06:31.520
of 10, 12, 14, 16 year olds marching.

01:06:31.520 --> 01:06:33.790
And what Bull Connor did, he
sprayed them with water hoses.

01:06:33.790 --> 01:06:36.020
The next time he released dogs on them.

01:06:36.020 --> 01:06:38.400
But suddenly, people sitting home in Iowa,

01:06:38.400 --> 01:06:41.431
in New Jersey, saw this
spectacle going on.

01:06:41.431 --> 01:06:44.180
Literally, the Soviet Union was making fun

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:46.870
of our democracy on the front
pages of their newspapers.

01:06:46.870 --> 01:06:50.130
And it so awoke that reservoir
of love in this country,

01:06:50.130 --> 01:06:52.610
within days, segregation
fell in Birmingham.

01:06:52.610 --> 01:06:55.620
Because this country, when
they decide to do something,

01:06:55.620 --> 01:06:58.140
nothing can stand in our way.

01:06:58.140 --> 01:07:01.385
And so the challenge is, now--
(applause)

01:07:01.385 --> 01:07:03.660
- It just sounds like you
gotta sink really low,

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:04.550
before you do something.

01:07:04.550 --> 01:07:07.410
- I think what we need to
do is find creative ways,

01:07:07.410 --> 01:07:09.787
I mean, you jokingly
said, SnapChat about it,

01:07:09.787 --> 01:07:12.280
but I'm sorry, I've done the political--

01:07:12.280 --> 01:07:13.150
- You should do it.

01:07:13.150 --> 01:07:16.240
- Yes. I've done the
political science research

01:07:16.240 --> 01:07:18.530
about what influences people to act.

01:07:18.530 --> 01:07:20.820
And did you know the most persuasive thing

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:23.340
to get your friends to vote is knowing

01:07:23.340 --> 01:07:26.140
if they're voting or
not. Is literally talking

01:07:26.140 --> 01:07:27.480
to your circle of friends.

01:07:27.480 --> 01:07:29.820
More than one of my campaign
commercials in New Jersey,

01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:33.227
if somebody says, "Hey,
everybody, I met Cory Booker,

01:07:33.227 --> 01:07:35.020
"he's a great guy, vote for him."

01:07:35.020 --> 01:07:37.840
That's far more persuasive
than anything I could put

01:07:37.840 --> 01:07:40.850
on TV or anything I can
do. We have so much power.

01:07:40.850 --> 01:07:42.600
And so this is my thing.
I don't think we need

01:07:42.600 --> 01:07:45.232
to light rivers on fire--

01:07:45.232 --> 01:07:46.200
- That was his idea.

01:07:46.200 --> 01:07:47.680
- I'm not crazy.

01:07:47.680 --> 01:07:48.513
- Whose idea was that?

01:07:48.513 --> 01:07:50.710
- It was the Russian that said that.

01:07:50.710 --> 01:07:53.630
- I don't think we need to do
it, I think it's effective.

01:07:53.630 --> 01:07:55.670
(laughter)

01:07:55.670 --> 01:07:58.700
- What we need to do is
ignite our own spirits.

01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:02.470
And I promise you, that light
will cast away some darkness.

01:08:02.470 --> 01:08:05.167
I just think we all need to
say, "What can I do different

01:08:05.167 --> 01:08:07.630
"this year, around issues
that I care about?"

01:08:07.630 --> 01:08:10.750
Whether it's science, or
Superfunds, or space exploration,

01:08:10.750 --> 01:08:15.600
pick something, and be a patriot with love

01:08:15.600 --> 01:08:18.870
in pursuit of that end.
And you will make more

01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:20.903
of a change than you could ever imagine.

01:08:21.795 --> 01:08:24.045
(applause)

01:08:25.780 --> 01:08:27.814
- Count Basie Theater, give it to me!

01:08:27.814 --> 01:08:30.545
(applause)

01:08:30.545 --> 01:08:32.717
We are live, at Count Basie
Theater, Red Bank, New Jersey!

01:08:34.617 --> 01:08:36.678
(applause)

01:08:36.678 --> 01:08:37.511
StarTalk!

01:08:39.360 --> 01:08:41.543
Let's make America smart again!

01:08:43.814 --> 01:08:44.647
All right.

01:08:48.250 --> 01:08:52.770
So, what I wanna know now is beyond.

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:57.430
Do we have the policy in
place to invent the future.

01:08:57.430 --> 01:09:00.310
Or again, are we only
reacting to bad things

01:09:00.310 --> 01:09:02.100
that have happened in the past.

01:09:02.100 --> 01:09:05.480
So John, let me begin
with you. How much duties

01:09:05.480 --> 01:09:08.730
of your office was to have
people think about tomorrow?

01:09:08.730 --> 01:09:11.026
- Well, a lot. And in fact,

01:09:11.026 --> 01:09:14.310
you have on your list space exploration.

01:09:14.310 --> 01:09:17.080
When we entered office,
we knew we had a challenge

01:09:17.080 --> 01:09:19.970
in space exploration
because a lot of the science

01:09:19.970 --> 01:09:22.750
had gone out of NASA, a lot
of the advanced technology

01:09:22.750 --> 01:09:26.070
had gone out of NASA, we
had to rebalance NASA.

01:09:26.070 --> 01:09:27.640
We said we were putting the science back

01:09:27.640 --> 01:09:29.153
in rocket science, in fact.

01:09:30.152 --> 01:09:31.747
- Did it work?

01:09:31.747 --> 01:09:33.330
- And we had a bit of a struggle?

01:09:33.330 --> 01:09:34.362
- That's a no.

01:09:34.362 --> 01:09:35.430
(laughter)

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:37.666
- It worked, I'm sorry, it worked.

01:09:37.666 --> 01:09:38.499
- Okay, thank you.

01:09:38.499 --> 01:09:41.110
- We did rebalance NASA,
and a lot of the stuff got--

01:09:41.110 --> 01:09:43.150
- Just to be clear, you were
in Washington for 8 years.

01:09:43.150 --> 01:09:44.170
- 8 years.

01:09:44.170 --> 01:09:47.090
- That's like longer than
any science advisor ever,

01:09:47.090 --> 01:09:49.000
in the history of the universe.

01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:51.420
- Well, of course the history
of the science advisors

01:09:51.420 --> 01:09:54.930
doesn't go back quite as far
as the history of the universe.

01:09:54.930 --> 01:09:59.550
It goes back to the last
term of, or the second

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:02.170
to last term of Franklin D. Roosevelt.

01:10:02.170 --> 01:10:04.320
But I was the longest
serving science advisor

01:10:04.320 --> 01:10:06.108
in that period.
(applause)

01:10:06.108 --> 01:10:06.941
- Okay.

01:10:08.710 --> 01:10:10.063
So you had perspective?

01:10:11.430 --> 01:10:14.070
- Well, sure. And of
course, like everybody else,

01:10:14.070 --> 01:10:16.210
and like you, I had been watching.

01:10:16.210 --> 01:10:18.720
I was a space geek when
I was a little kid.

01:10:18.720 --> 01:10:20.970
I was making solid fuel
rockets out of my mother's

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:22.857
used lipstick tubes when I was 9.

01:10:22.857 --> 01:10:23.900
(laughter)

01:10:23.900 --> 01:10:28.900
They went about 100 feet
in the air, but yeah.

01:10:28.960 --> 01:10:30.560
I've been watching it for a long time.

01:10:30.560 --> 01:10:33.130
And it was a pleasure
to have the opportunity,

01:10:33.130 --> 01:10:34.780
working with President Obama and working

01:10:34.780 --> 01:10:36.682
with Charlie Bolton,
the NASA administrator,

01:10:36.682 --> 01:10:37.674
to get things done.

01:10:37.674 --> 01:10:38.590
- You made rockets out of lipstick tubes.

01:10:38.590 --> 01:10:40.630
- When I was nine I did that.
- Did that hurt your

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:42.094
background check, when
they did a background check

01:10:42.094 --> 01:10:43.790
and they found out that you
blew things up as a kid.

01:10:43.790 --> 01:10:45.270
- Well, yeah. That was a bit of a problem.

01:10:45.270 --> 01:10:47.592
But they decided to let me through.

01:10:47.592 --> 01:10:48.425
- Got one of those waivers.

01:10:48.425 --> 01:10:50.140
- You made rockets out of lipstick tubes.

01:10:50.140 --> 01:10:52.900
- Little solid fuel rockets.
Yeah, I had chemistry set

01:10:52.900 --> 01:10:55.260
ingredients that made the solid fuel.

01:10:55.260 --> 01:10:58.120
I made time fuse, burned an inch a minute,

01:10:58.120 --> 01:11:00.810
so I could get away before it went off.

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:03.400
- Next time I see six
year old boys in Sephora,

01:11:03.400 --> 01:11:04.593
I'll know what they're up to.

01:11:04.593 --> 01:11:06.843
(laughter)

01:11:09.143 --> 01:11:11.865
- 101 things to do with lipstick tubes.

01:11:11.865 --> 01:11:12.698
- That's amazing.

01:11:12.698 --> 01:11:15.106
- Okay, I'm sorry. I was very
distracted by that little bit.

01:11:15.106 --> 01:11:17.640
- No, I apologize, it was my fault.

01:11:17.640 --> 01:11:19.764
- Yeah, okay I think I'm
back on track. Okay go on.

01:11:19.764 --> 01:11:24.764
- We did get a lot done in
reshaping the priorities in NASA.

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:28.240
More investments in the
technology that would be needed

01:11:28.240 --> 01:11:29.280
to go to Mars.

01:11:29.280 --> 01:11:30.610
You know, a lot of people
are saying why don't we go

01:11:30.610 --> 01:11:32.770
to Mars tomorrow? Let's put the money in,

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:35.710
and you know Neil, as well
as I do, that we don't yet

01:11:35.710 --> 01:11:38.430
have the technologies to
send people to the surface

01:11:38.430 --> 01:11:39.960
of Mars and bring them back.

01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:41.110
Of course, there are some who are willing

01:11:41.110 --> 01:11:42.493
to take a one-way trip.

01:11:43.960 --> 01:11:45.640
And some others who would be my candidates

01:11:45.640 --> 01:11:46.668
for a one-way trip.

01:11:46.668 --> 01:11:48.484
- We have a one-way trip right here.

01:11:48.484 --> 01:11:49.840
(laughter and applause)

01:11:49.840 --> 01:11:53.230
- So, your citing NASA,
in response to my question

01:11:53.230 --> 01:11:55.947
about the future, is NASA the repository

01:11:55.947 --> 01:11:59.069
of our future hopes among
agencies in the government?

01:11:59.069 --> 01:12:01.252
- No, it's only one. It just happens to be

01:12:01.252 --> 01:12:04.090
a particularly evocative
one, and one that still

01:12:04.090 --> 01:12:06.873
by the way, inspires
young people in the way

01:12:06.873 --> 01:12:09.160
that nothing else in science does.

01:12:09.160 --> 01:12:11.900
At the big science fairs
that we've had in Washington,

01:12:11.900 --> 01:12:15.290
the two exhibits that always
attract the most attention

01:12:15.290 --> 01:12:19.710
are NASA and robots. Those
are the two that really do it,

01:12:19.710 --> 01:12:22.830
that get kids going about
science and technology.

01:12:22.830 --> 01:12:26.420
- So, how do you draw the
line between the research

01:12:26.420 --> 01:12:28.240
you do that helps invent the future,

01:12:28.240 --> 01:12:30.530
and the research that
Congress will tell you

01:12:30.530 --> 01:12:32.690
you shouldn't be doing,
because corporations

01:12:32.690 --> 01:12:35.140
should be doing that as part of their R&amp;D.

01:12:35.140 --> 01:12:36.510
Where's that line?

01:12:36.510 --> 01:12:38.040
It's gotta be somewhere in there.

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:40.210
- Well, there is the fairly obvious line.

01:12:40.210 --> 01:12:43.810
In fact, the corporate sector funds more

01:12:43.810 --> 01:12:46.410
than two-thirds of all
the R&amp;D in this country,

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:49.660
but they fund less than
half of the basic research.

01:12:49.660 --> 01:12:52.280
The fundamental research
that's a seed corn

01:12:52.280 --> 01:12:53.476
from which all the future applied--

01:12:53.476 --> 01:12:54.830
- The long horizon research.

01:12:54.830 --> 01:12:56.840
- And the reason the private
sector doesn't do that

01:12:56.840 --> 01:12:58.370
is perfectly understandable.

01:12:58.370 --> 01:13:01.460
Time horizon is too long,
the risk is too high,

01:13:01.460 --> 01:13:05.180
the return is too uncertain,
and they're not sure

01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:08.460
that if there's a breakthrough
from this basic research,

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:10.023
that they, the corporation
that paid for it,

01:13:10.023 --> 01:13:11.950
will get the benefits.

01:13:11.950 --> 01:13:13.050
- But I always hear in Congress--

01:13:13.050 --> 01:13:14.580
- The government needs
to do it. The government

01:13:14.580 --> 01:13:16.610
needs to do that sort of basic research,

01:13:16.610 --> 01:13:18.190
needs to fund it, or it won't get done.

01:13:18.190 --> 01:13:21.420
- Yeah, when that happens,
Congress stands up

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:24.777
and says, "Why is tax
payer money being wasted

01:13:24.777 --> 01:13:27.527
"on this research that has no application

01:13:27.527 --> 01:13:29.583
"to any known thing on Earth?"

01:13:29.583 --> 01:13:32.635
- What are examples of some
of this terrible research?

01:13:32.635 --> 01:13:34.440
- Yeah, I'll give you some.

01:13:34.440 --> 01:13:36.610
The nature of basic
research is you can't tell

01:13:36.610 --> 01:13:37.670
where it's gonna go.

01:13:37.670 --> 01:13:41.500
Great example, Charles Townes
, who got the Nobel Prize

01:13:41.500 --> 01:13:44.640
for thinking up the
science behind the maser

01:13:44.640 --> 01:13:49.000
and then the laser, had no
idea when he did that work,

01:13:49.000 --> 01:13:51.530
that 50 years later,
lasers would be the way

01:13:51.530 --> 01:13:55.130
we do eye surgery, cut metal,
copy documents, play movies,

01:13:55.130 --> 01:13:58.610
major distances, none of that was obvious

01:13:58.610 --> 01:14:00.430
at the time the work was done.

01:14:00.430 --> 01:14:03.390
- We even measured distance
to the moon with lasers.

01:14:03.390 --> 01:14:07.750
- I think lasers are
worth it, just my opinion.

01:14:07.750 --> 01:14:09.510
- There's another great example.

01:14:09.510 --> 01:14:11.810
There was a science project funded by the

01:14:11.810 --> 01:14:14.210
National Science
Foundation many years ago,

01:14:14.210 --> 01:14:16.910
was called The Sex Life of the Screw Worm.

01:14:16.910 --> 01:14:17.743
- Yeah.

01:14:19.550 --> 01:14:21.660
- Sex Life of the Screw Worm.

01:14:21.660 --> 01:14:22.790
- That's a real worm?

01:14:22.790 --> 01:14:24.192
- Real worm.

01:14:24.192 --> 01:14:26.521
- Guess what it does.

01:14:26.521 --> 01:14:27.873
(laughter)

01:14:27.873 --> 01:14:30.930
- And a lot of fun was made
of this in the Congress,

01:14:30.930 --> 01:14:33.770
I think it got Senator's
Proxmire's Golden Fleece Award,

01:14:33.770 --> 01:14:34.893
in fact--

01:14:34.893 --> 01:14:39.350
- The award given to the greatest
waste of taxpayer's money?

01:14:39.350 --> 01:14:42.050
- And the fact is, the screw
worm was a livestock pass

01:14:42.050 --> 01:14:44.300
that did some hundred million
dollars worth of damage

01:14:44.300 --> 01:14:47.490
every year to the livestock
industry in the United States.

01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:51.130
And this basic research, on
the sex life of the screw worm,

01:14:51.130 --> 01:14:54.480
led to a means of biological
control of the screw worm,

01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:57.840
which basically eradicated
it as a livestock pass.

01:14:57.840 --> 01:15:00.860
With immense savings to the US economy.

01:15:00.860 --> 01:15:02.530
- Was that just a
marketing failure though?

01:15:02.530 --> 01:15:03.790
Like shouldn't it have been called like

01:15:03.790 --> 01:15:06.737
Save Our Agriculture Business research?

01:15:06.737 --> 01:15:08.391
You can't vote against that.

01:15:08.391 --> 01:15:11.183
- The people doing the
research didn't know

01:15:11.183 --> 01:15:13.600
that that would be the
outcome, that's the nature

01:15:13.600 --> 01:15:14.633
of basic research.

01:15:14.633 --> 01:15:17.720
- And then they put condoms
on the screw worms--

01:15:17.720 --> 01:15:20.903
- Safe sex for screw worms,
better agriculture for America.

01:15:20.903 --> 01:15:22.550
(laughter)

01:15:22.550 --> 01:15:24.670
- The solution was actually
somewhat similar to that.

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:25.503
- Really?

01:15:25.503 --> 01:15:27.340
- The solution was
releasing sterile males,

01:15:27.340 --> 01:15:29.630
'cuz it turns out that the
screw worm only mated once,

01:15:29.630 --> 01:15:31.290
the female only mated once.

01:15:31.290 --> 01:15:33.790
And if the female mated
with a sterile male--

01:15:33.790 --> 01:15:34.973
- She was done.

01:15:34.973 --> 01:15:36.860
- Then, no offspring. And so the idea--

01:15:36.860 --> 01:15:37.960
- And she still had a good tush.

01:15:37.960 --> 01:15:40.057
- If you just release a ton--

01:15:40.057 --> 01:15:42.196
(laughter)

01:15:42.196 --> 01:15:44.600
you just release a ton of sterile males,

01:15:44.600 --> 01:15:47.445
and the screw worm goes out of business.

01:15:47.445 --> 01:15:48.278
- That's brilliant.

01:15:48.278 --> 01:15:49.652
- I can't believe we
just spent ten minutes

01:15:49.652 --> 01:15:50.488
talking about the screw worm.

01:15:50.488 --> 01:15:52.197
- They do the same thing
at Skidmore actually.

01:15:52.197 --> 01:15:53.030
(laughter)

01:15:53.030 --> 01:15:53.863
- I apologize.

01:15:53.863 --> 01:15:55.060
- No, that's fine.

01:15:55.060 --> 01:15:56.090
- You asked for an example.

01:15:56.090 --> 01:16:00.180
- But we know Cory, that
there are people, in Congress,

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:04.870
both sides of the branches,
that don't appreciate this.

01:16:04.870 --> 01:16:06.500
- Absolutely. There's people that don't.

01:16:06.500 --> 01:16:09.610
- How do we get them to appreciate it?

01:16:09.610 --> 01:16:12.250
- Again, that's the political process.

01:16:12.250 --> 01:16:13.870
That's the sort of sausage making,

01:16:13.870 --> 01:16:16.542
or screw worm funding process.

01:16:16.542 --> 01:16:18.780
- It's not just education, it's
not just examples like this.

01:16:18.780 --> 01:16:19.613
- No it's not.

01:16:19.613 --> 01:16:22.040
- Why can't he stand up,
and give that example,

01:16:22.040 --> 01:16:26.260
I give three others, and
these are tangible examples.

01:16:26.260 --> 01:16:27.820
Why doesn't that convince people?

01:16:27.820 --> 01:16:30.913
Is there missing part of
the K through 12 education

01:16:30.913 --> 01:16:35.190
where the receptors
aren't there for examples

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:37.240
that might change their mind?

01:16:37.240 --> 01:16:40.580
- Again, this is a process
in which there's tons

01:16:40.580 --> 01:16:43.030
of competing demands, and there are people

01:16:43.030 --> 01:16:45.500
that are dead set against
this kind of science research.

01:16:45.500 --> 01:16:46.930
And don't get the larger picture.

01:16:46.930 --> 01:16:49.530
- Is it 'cuz they dug in
their heels, and that's it?

01:16:50.570 --> 01:16:51.807
- With respect Neil--

01:16:51.807 --> 01:16:53.440
- No, I don't ever want you to respect me,

01:16:53.440 --> 01:16:56.440
just bring it out. I'll
take care of you later.

01:16:56.440 --> 01:16:58.983
- Then with extreme disrespect,

01:16:59.960 --> 01:17:02.700
you're coming at this as a scientist,

01:17:02.700 --> 01:17:04.710
and you're leaning on
these facts as if facts

01:17:04.710 --> 01:17:06.560
have ever always been enough.

01:17:06.560 --> 01:17:10.271
Any parent knows that you
tell the kid a fact once,

01:17:10.271 --> 01:17:12.410
why do they keep misbehaving? I told them,

01:17:12.410 --> 01:17:14.830
if this happens, this'll be
the consequence. But we do it.

01:17:14.830 --> 01:17:16.780
'Cuz we have emotions,
and we have tribalism,

01:17:16.780 --> 01:17:18.320
and we wanna feel a sense of belonging.

01:17:18.320 --> 01:17:20.530
So I think some of these
reasons that people

01:17:20.530 --> 01:17:23.590
are being obstinate, information
alone is never enough

01:17:23.590 --> 01:17:24.790
to close a case.

01:17:24.790 --> 01:17:26.850
And so it's an important first step,

01:17:26.850 --> 01:17:29.254
but you gotta build some
layers on top of that.

01:17:29.254 --> 01:17:33.082
- Okay, in the day, it was
called an ass whoopin'.

01:17:33.082 --> 01:17:34.920
(laughter)

01:17:34.920 --> 01:17:35.950
That's how you convince someone,

01:17:35.950 --> 01:17:37.650
if the data didn't otherwise work.

01:17:39.653 --> 01:17:42.590
I'm just curious about that.
There are other branches

01:17:42.590 --> 01:17:45.710
of the government other than NASA.

01:17:45.710 --> 01:17:47.670
I don't know if they
were in your portfolio,

01:17:47.670 --> 01:17:49.790
but DARPA is something
we've always heard about.

01:17:49.790 --> 01:17:54.220
Defense Advance Research
Projects Administration.

01:17:54.220 --> 01:17:55.053
- Agency.

01:17:55.053 --> 01:17:55.886
- And there's DARPA-E.

01:17:55.886 --> 01:17:56.806
- Huh?

01:17:56.806 --> 01:17:57.639
- There's DARPA-E.

01:17:57.639 --> 01:17:58.472
Which is?

01:17:58.472 --> 01:17:59.305
which I'm very interested in.

01:17:59.305 --> 01:18:01.650
It's investments in alternative energy.

01:18:01.650 --> 01:18:03.180
- So E for energy.

01:18:03.180 --> 01:18:04.013
- Yes.

01:18:04.013 --> 01:18:06.980
- So these are funded by
the Department of Defense?

01:18:06.980 --> 01:18:07.813
- Yes.

01:18:07.813 --> 01:18:08.646
- Okay.

01:18:08.646 --> 01:18:10.100
- DARPA-E is funded by
the Department of Energy.

01:18:10.100 --> 01:18:14.260
- Okay, but neither of them
are in OSTP's portfolio.

01:18:14.260 --> 01:18:15.390
- Oh they are.
- Oh they are.

01:18:15.390 --> 01:18:18.508
- OSTP has oversight of all
the science and technology--

01:18:18.508 --> 01:18:19.548
- No matter who's doin' it.

01:18:19.548 --> 01:18:20.450
- No matter who's doin' it.

01:18:20.450 --> 01:18:21.283
- Oh, okay.

01:18:22.230 --> 01:18:26.110
- And we work together with
the departments and agencies

01:18:26.110 --> 01:18:28.367
in developing the president's budget

01:18:28.367 --> 01:18:30.180
for science and technology.
- So tell me about robots.

01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:32.180
You said robots get everybody's attention

01:18:32.180 --> 01:18:33.238
at the science fairs.

01:18:33.238 --> 01:18:34.071
- Oh absolutely.

01:18:34.071 --> 01:18:35.930
- And I know DARPA's
been making some robots.

01:18:35.930 --> 01:18:37.040
- Absolutely.

01:18:37.040 --> 01:18:37.873
- What?

01:18:39.060 --> 01:18:39.893
- Yeah.

01:18:39.893 --> 01:18:42.043
(laughter)

01:18:42.043 --> 01:18:43.943
You made it sound naughty, but anyway.

01:18:44.950 --> 01:18:47.150
Would it help to just
reframe all our science

01:18:47.150 --> 01:18:48.053
as a weapon?

01:18:48.920 --> 01:18:52.300
- Yes. But look, I mean one of the reasons

01:18:52.300 --> 01:18:53.663
why we can get a lot of
very good research done

01:18:53.663 --> 01:18:56.420
through the Department of
Defense, because it's often

01:18:56.420 --> 01:18:59.410
easier to get people to fund
the Department of Defense

01:18:59.410 --> 01:19:02.310
than it is to get them to fund
some of these other agencies.

01:19:02.310 --> 01:19:05.540
- Because they're evoking
the I don't wanna die urge.

01:19:05.540 --> 01:19:08.450
- If the screw worm was a
weapon, there'd be no problem.

01:19:08.450 --> 01:19:11.130
- Right. There is a
battle going on right now

01:19:11.130 --> 01:19:15.140
about defense spending
versus domestic spending.

01:19:15.140 --> 01:19:17.800
And this idea of should there
be parity in the increases

01:19:17.800 --> 01:19:19.350
and the like?

01:19:19.350 --> 01:19:21.790
But I just have a question,
because I got two scientists

01:19:21.790 --> 01:19:23.123
here and it's something
I've read a lot about,

01:19:23.123 --> 01:19:26.460
when you talk about
larger planetary threats.

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:30.180
Isn't there a real threat
of an e-impulse for example,

01:19:30.180 --> 01:19:31.850
a naturally occurring one

01:19:31.850 --> 01:19:35.050
that could really knock out
America's infrastructure.

01:19:35.050 --> 01:19:37.010
- Yes, is the short answer.

01:19:37.010 --> 01:19:38.090
- You happy now?

01:19:38.090 --> 01:19:40.510
- I'm not happy, I'm one of these people

01:19:40.510 --> 01:19:41.840
that wants to see more infrastructure--

01:19:41.840 --> 01:19:44.080
- Tell everyone about the e-impulse.

01:19:44.080 --> 01:19:45.945
- Wait, is "The Matrix" real?

01:19:45.945 --> 01:19:48.470
(laughter)

01:19:48.470 --> 01:19:52.310
- Right, matrix had an
e-impulse to get rid of the--

01:19:52.310 --> 01:19:53.706
- Robot squids that eat people.

01:19:53.706 --> 01:19:55.750
- The squidy things, is
that what they're called?

01:19:55.750 --> 01:19:58.250
- The Sentinels, you guys
didn't see "The Matrix?"

01:19:59.400 --> 01:20:00.705
The Sentinels.

01:20:00.705 --> 01:20:01.980
- So there are two kinds
of electromagnetic pulse.

01:20:01.980 --> 01:20:06.340
One is if you explode a
nuclear bomb in the atmosphere,

01:20:06.340 --> 01:20:08.460
among many other things
it generates a pulse

01:20:08.460 --> 01:20:11.150
of electromagnetic energy that can fry

01:20:11.150 --> 01:20:14.370
the electronics in your
cellphone, in your car's ignition,

01:20:14.370 --> 01:20:17.650
in the controllers of the
electricity grid, and so on.

01:20:17.650 --> 01:20:19.170
So that's one of the many good reasons

01:20:19.170 --> 01:20:21.520
not to explode nuclear
weapons in the atmosphere.

01:20:21.520 --> 01:20:22.979
- Is that it will ruin your phone.

01:20:22.979 --> 01:20:25.598
- It will ruin your phone. (laughter)

01:20:25.598 --> 01:20:30.431
- And it would be ruined, like
you would need a new phone.

01:20:31.827 --> 01:20:34.597
- Didn't the Nokia 7 have
one of these problems?

01:20:34.597 --> 01:20:35.430
- Yeah.

01:20:35.430 --> 01:20:36.592
- No, Samsung 7.

01:20:36.592 --> 01:20:37.425
- Samsung 7.

01:20:37.425 --> 01:20:40.400
- But the natural version
of an electromagnetic pulse

01:20:40.400 --> 01:20:44.290
is when a solar storm--

01:20:44.290 --> 01:20:45.123
- A solar flare.

01:20:45.123 --> 01:20:48.100
- A solar flare, throws charge particles

01:20:48.100 --> 01:20:50.810
in the direction of the
Earth, and they interact

01:20:50.810 --> 01:20:53.940
with the Earth's magnetic
field, in a way that generates

01:20:53.940 --> 01:20:56.930
a pulse of electromagnetic
energy at the surface.

01:20:56.930 --> 01:21:01.890
And that too, can fry your
phone, your electricity grid,

01:21:01.890 --> 01:21:02.748
and everything else.

01:21:02.748 --> 01:21:03.980
- This has happened?

01:21:03.980 --> 01:21:05.120
- It has happened.

01:21:05.120 --> 01:21:06.284
- On a massive scale.

01:21:06.284 --> 01:21:07.279
- It happened in Canada.

01:21:07.279 --> 01:21:08.112
- Thank goodness.

01:21:08.112 --> 01:21:09.384
- It's happening in modern times,

01:21:09.384 --> 01:21:11.508
it happened in a part of Canada.

01:21:11.508 --> 01:21:13.774
But it also happened,
there was an event in the

01:21:13.774 --> 01:21:15.750
late 19th century--
- 1860 right?

01:21:15.750 --> 01:21:20.750
- Yeah. That was so severe,
it knocked out telegraph

01:21:21.270 --> 01:21:22.890
over a very large area. But there wasn't

01:21:22.890 --> 01:21:25.516
much electrical equipment in those days.

01:21:25.516 --> 01:21:26.349
- Right.

01:21:26.349 --> 01:21:28.050
- And so it didn't do that much damage.

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:30.750
- But we know that if an
event of that magnitude

01:21:30.750 --> 01:21:33.720
occurred today, it would be devastating.

01:21:33.720 --> 01:21:34.980
- It could cripple our country.

01:21:34.980 --> 01:21:36.510
- So we're at war with the sun.

01:21:36.510 --> 01:21:39.000
- As a result of that possibility,

01:21:39.000 --> 01:21:42.230
we have invested now substantial effort

01:21:42.230 --> 01:21:45.970
in trying to build a multi-prong strategy

01:21:45.970 --> 01:21:48.720
to protect us from those kinds of events.

01:21:48.720 --> 01:21:52.010
That strategy includes sensors
on the Discover satellite,

01:21:52.010 --> 01:21:54.800
to give us early warning.
The strategy includes

01:21:54.800 --> 01:21:58.150
the ability to disconnect
parts of the electricity grid

01:21:58.150 --> 01:22:01.040
on warning very quickly.
But there are other things

01:22:01.040 --> 01:22:04.160
that we should be doing, and
that the study recommended

01:22:04.160 --> 01:22:06.480
that we do, that we're not yet doing.

01:22:06.480 --> 01:22:07.313
- Right.

01:22:07.313 --> 01:22:08.218
- And that's something that

01:22:08.218 --> 01:22:09.051
I'm very glad you're interested in.

01:22:09.051 --> 01:22:10.970
- No, this is my point,
is the things that I read,

01:22:10.970 --> 01:22:13.260
there's too much that
I read that worry me--

01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:14.796
- Okay, that's not all you
should be worrying about.

01:22:14.796 --> 01:22:17.118
(laughter)

01:22:17.118 --> 01:22:19.520
- Well, we should be worrying
about that as a globe.

01:22:19.520 --> 01:22:20.829
- Yeah, when's this happening?

01:22:20.829 --> 01:22:22.000
(laughter)

01:22:22.000 --> 01:22:22.833
- We don't know.

01:22:22.833 --> 01:22:24.030
- Does it detect Mardi Gras?

01:22:24.030 --> 01:22:24.993
- Not predictable.

01:22:26.210 --> 01:22:29.840
- So there's not only
that, there's all this talk

01:22:29.840 --> 01:22:31.840
about AI running amuck.

01:22:31.840 --> 01:22:35.120
And does the United States
have a major investment

01:22:35.120 --> 01:22:37.000
in this, the future of this technology?

01:22:37.000 --> 01:22:37.833
- So we've basically gone

01:22:37.833 --> 01:22:40.280
from "The Matrix" to
"Terminator" now, but keep going.

01:22:40.280 --> 01:22:41.183
- Yeah.

01:22:41.183 --> 01:22:42.016
(laughter)

01:22:42.016 --> 01:22:42.849
- Both happy movies.

01:22:44.420 --> 01:22:47.340
- In my regular life, about
how scared should I really be?

01:22:47.340 --> 01:22:51.410
Like a one out of ten? Like a six?

01:22:51.410 --> 01:22:53.680
- So AI, let me ask you this, all right.

01:22:53.680 --> 01:22:57.560
We had Ray Kurzweil as
a guest on StarTalk.

01:22:57.560 --> 01:22:59.350
And I was delighted by that conversation,

01:22:59.350 --> 01:23:00.410
because I had only known of him

01:23:00.410 --> 01:23:01.850
from what other people wrote.

01:23:01.850 --> 01:23:03.531
You know, I finally got the horse's mouth,

01:23:03.531 --> 01:23:06.570
and I love the guy to no end.

01:23:06.570 --> 01:23:08.760
Just, he's a deep thinker, he's brilliant,

01:23:08.760 --> 01:23:11.260
and so what I ask, there's a lot of talk

01:23:11.260 --> 01:23:15.810
about connecting human
biology to the Internet

01:23:15.810 --> 01:23:19.510
in some way, so that your
brain is now actively

01:23:19.510 --> 01:23:24.320
processing the world. And,
do you see this biologically

01:23:24.320 --> 01:23:26.900
as a real thing coming down the line?

01:23:26.900 --> 01:23:28.220
- I think so, I don't think it's--

01:23:28.220 --> 01:23:29.053
- Yes?

01:23:29.053 --> 01:23:30.560
- Yes, I don't think it's imminent.

01:23:30.560 --> 01:23:34.460
I think that's a way, ways off.

01:23:34.460 --> 01:23:37.800
- But we're steps there
already. There's biologics

01:23:37.800 --> 01:23:39.810
that you can put inside
yourself that you can feel

01:23:39.810 --> 01:23:42.840
a monitor, distribute
medicine, your doctor

01:23:42.840 --> 01:23:45.680
could literally sit at a
computer, we're getting

01:23:45.680 --> 01:23:48.010
close to that, and be able to
deliver you doses of medicine

01:23:48.010 --> 01:23:50.040
based upon the information they're getting

01:23:50.040 --> 01:23:52.480
over distances about what's
happening inside your body.

01:23:52.480 --> 01:23:53.620
- That's precision medicine,

01:23:53.620 --> 01:23:55.916
we were talking about it before.

01:23:55.916 --> 01:24:00.916
- But AI, now is making decisions
that I didn't authorize.

01:24:01.032 --> 01:24:06.032
Right? So the big fear is that
AI, and I tweeted this once,

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:09.557
I said, "We better behave.
Because when AI achieves

01:24:09.557 --> 01:24:12.357
"consciousness, we wanna
give it as fewer reasons

01:24:12.357 --> 01:24:14.730
"as possible to exterminate us."

01:24:14.730 --> 01:24:15.723
Okay?

01:24:16.954 --> 01:24:19.870
- People are clapping
for termination by AI.

01:24:19.870 --> 01:24:21.040
- No, I think they agree. Right?

01:24:21.040 --> 01:24:22.934
- Like don't worry, autos will save us.

01:24:22.934 --> 01:24:24.440
(laughter)

01:24:24.440 --> 01:24:27.103
- So, is there an agency
that's thinking about AI.

01:24:28.060 --> 01:24:29.801
- That's John's area.

01:24:29.801 --> 01:24:33.910
- Yeah, I mean we had many,
many meetings in the White House

01:24:33.910 --> 01:24:36.510
about AI, many of them
including President Obama,

01:24:36.510 --> 01:24:39.100
who's very interested in
it and concerned about it.

01:24:39.100 --> 01:24:39.933
- Did he write a paper on it?

01:24:39.933 --> 01:24:44.569
- It has an upside, in terms of increasing

01:24:44.569 --> 01:24:47.220
the capacity to get a lot
of important things done.

01:24:47.220 --> 01:24:49.310
And has a downside, like many technologies

01:24:49.310 --> 01:24:52.200
if it's misused, if it
evolves in a bad direction,

01:24:52.200 --> 01:24:54.400
it could be problematic.
And so the question is,

01:24:54.400 --> 01:24:57.900
how do you manage the
evolution of this technology

01:24:57.900 --> 01:24:59.750
in a way that gets the benefits

01:24:59.750 --> 01:25:01.990
while minimizing the dangers.

01:25:01.990 --> 01:25:05.760
But, my own view, is that the dangers,

01:25:05.760 --> 01:25:10.400
as we currently understand
them, are being overstated.

01:25:10.400 --> 01:25:15.060
The proposition that
computers are going to become

01:25:15.060 --> 01:25:17.810
in some general sense,
smarter than humans,

01:25:17.810 --> 01:25:22.500
sometime soon, is not believed
by many of the experts

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:24.980
in the field. There's some
who think it will happen.

01:25:24.980 --> 01:25:26.550
There are many that think it won't happen.

01:25:26.550 --> 01:25:28.400
That doesn't mean we shouldn't be vigilant

01:25:28.400 --> 01:25:32.070
in trying to figure out
how to make sure that AI

01:25:32.070 --> 01:25:34.460
doesn't evolve in a way
that takes over our lives.

01:25:34.460 --> 01:25:36.900
- But can I tell you where
AI scares me right now

01:25:36.900 --> 01:25:40.790
and is real? Is that our enemies,

01:25:40.790 --> 01:25:42.477
sorry Eugene, like Russia--

01:25:43.530 --> 01:25:46.265
- I'm from America. Just to be clear.

01:25:46.265 --> 01:25:48.450
(laughter)

01:25:48.450 --> 01:25:49.283
- Sorry to put you--

01:25:49.283 --> 01:25:51.133
- I mean I'll blend in if they win, but...

01:25:51.133 --> 01:25:53.383
(laughter)

01:25:58.600 --> 01:25:59.850
- So we have a real problem

01:25:59.850 --> 01:26:02.710
in this country with cyber attacks.

01:26:02.710 --> 01:26:05.500
And one of the areas
in which AI technology

01:26:05.500 --> 01:26:07.800
is now being used, look
Russia, China, will never

01:26:07.800 --> 01:26:10.800
beat us tank for tank,
war ship for war ship,

01:26:10.800 --> 01:26:13.060
we spend more money on
military, on bigger military,

01:26:13.060 --> 01:26:16.100
than the next 6, 7 countries combined.

01:26:16.100 --> 01:26:19.250
But where they can now offer
a threat, we just saw this

01:26:19.250 --> 01:26:23.920
with a massive cyber attack,
is with the advancements

01:26:23.920 --> 01:26:26.400
that are being made in hacking
and that kind of technology.

01:26:26.400 --> 01:26:30.940
And AI is being used,
invested in, and explored

01:26:30.940 --> 01:26:33.340
by the Chinese and the Russians, as a way

01:26:33.340 --> 01:26:35.160
of having a far more intelligent way,

01:26:35.160 --> 01:26:37.630
where the computer can
itself be the learner

01:26:37.630 --> 01:26:39.560
about what the defenses are of a system,

01:26:39.560 --> 01:26:40.980
and better break into them.

01:26:40.980 --> 01:26:44.290
And so, what you see are, our competitors,

01:26:44.290 --> 01:26:46.440
and remember it's not
just Russia, it's China,

01:26:46.440 --> 01:26:47.640
who's doing extraordinary jobs

01:26:47.640 --> 01:26:49.870
stealing business
technologies and the like,

01:26:49.870 --> 01:26:52.900
using these very
sophisticated AI, block chain

01:26:52.900 --> 01:26:56.340
all these new, next generation sort of

01:26:56.340 --> 01:26:58.810
technologies and innovations, against us,

01:26:58.810 --> 01:27:00.230
and beating us to the punch.

01:27:00.230 --> 01:27:02.370
It's a massive
vulnerability for our nation

01:27:02.370 --> 01:27:04.308
that we should be very
aware and talk about.

01:27:04.308 --> 01:27:06.824
- So that would come under
the Department of Defense.

01:27:06.824 --> 01:27:08.740
But the Department of
Defense, other than the DARPA

01:27:08.740 --> 01:27:11.670
and the DARPA-E, is
not really as equipped,

01:27:11.670 --> 01:27:16.280
it seems to me, to attract
the best and the brightest

01:27:16.280 --> 01:27:17.113
to solve that problem.

01:27:17.113 --> 01:27:19.220
- Well, I don't think that's quite right.

01:27:19.220 --> 01:27:20.450
The Department of Defense includes

01:27:20.450 --> 01:27:23.570
The National Security Agency,
National Security Agency

01:27:23.570 --> 01:27:25.860
employs more PhD mathematicians than any

01:27:25.860 --> 01:27:27.316
other organization in the world.

01:27:27.316 --> 01:27:28.149
- They got it.

01:27:28.149 --> 01:27:30.400
- They are thinking about AI extensively.

01:27:30.400 --> 01:27:34.380
As is DARPA, which has a
lot of smart folks as well.

01:27:34.380 --> 01:27:35.620
I'm not saying there's no problem,

01:27:35.620 --> 01:27:38.242
I agree with the Senator,
this is a big risk.

01:27:38.242 --> 01:27:40.737
It's a big area of competition.

01:27:40.737 --> 01:27:42.510
Our adversaries are very busy,

01:27:42.510 --> 01:27:44.010
we're very busy too, by the way.

01:27:44.010 --> 01:27:48.920
And AI can be used to
defend our cyber systems,

01:27:48.920 --> 01:27:52.040
just as it can be used by our
adversaries to attack them,

01:27:52.040 --> 01:27:53.140
so this is an ongoing--

01:27:53.140 --> 01:27:54.700
- So we could put our AI against their AI,

01:27:54.700 --> 01:27:57.418
and then just let them
fight and we go to the park,

01:27:57.418 --> 01:27:59.406
or something.
(laughter)

01:27:59.406 --> 01:28:01.656
- That would be good, yeah.

01:28:02.613 --> 01:28:05.660
- All right, let me just get
into your lives for a moment.

01:28:05.660 --> 01:28:08.730
Each of you left academic posts

01:28:08.730 --> 01:28:13.730
to serve in the White House.

01:28:13.850 --> 01:28:18.850
And you became sort of a
citizen scientists servant

01:28:20.610 --> 01:28:24.610
of needs. Each of you,
what drove you to do that?

01:28:24.610 --> 01:28:26.230
- Well for me, there were two factors.

01:28:26.230 --> 01:28:29.230
One was John Holdren, the
other was Barack Obama.

01:28:29.230 --> 01:28:31.627
- Okay, well--
(applause)

01:28:31.627 --> 01:28:33.900
- They were totally impressive intellects

01:28:33.900 --> 01:28:37.310
committed to science and
I think John convinced me

01:28:37.310 --> 01:28:39.100
when we first talked about the position,

01:28:39.100 --> 01:28:41.970
that working for this
president would be a privilege

01:28:41.970 --> 01:28:45.070
beyond all else for a
scientist. And he was right.

01:28:45.070 --> 01:28:47.800
It was an honor to work
for a president who cared

01:28:47.800 --> 01:28:49.484
so much about science.

01:28:49.484 --> 01:28:51.734
(applause)

01:28:54.629 --> 01:28:58.060
- So 9 years ago, what were you thinking?

01:28:58.060 --> 01:29:00.290
- Well, first of all, I
had had the good fortune

01:29:00.290 --> 01:29:03.560
to meet President Eisenhower's second term

01:29:03.560 --> 01:29:07.970
science advisor George Kistiakowsky
when I was 29 years old.

01:29:07.970 --> 01:29:09.960
And he became one of my
mentors, and I learned

01:29:09.960 --> 01:29:13.620
a lot from him about his
service for Eisenhower.

01:29:13.620 --> 01:29:17.010
Then I met Jerry Wiesner, who
was JFK's science advisor,

01:29:17.010 --> 01:29:19.840
and he became a mentor.
And I ended up knowing

01:29:19.840 --> 01:29:22.390
every science advisor
to every US president

01:29:22.390 --> 01:29:24.530
from Eisenhower on--

01:29:24.530 --> 01:29:25.560
- So if you didn't get that job--

01:29:25.560 --> 01:29:28.430
- So I had a secret ambition as a result

01:29:28.430 --> 01:29:29.980
of all those interactions that I might

01:29:29.980 --> 01:29:32.390
someday be a president's science advisor,

01:29:32.390 --> 01:29:35.130
and I just happened to
luck out and get the best

01:29:35.130 --> 01:29:38.671
president in modern times,
to be the science advisor to.

01:29:38.671 --> 01:29:40.921
(applause)

01:29:42.090 --> 01:29:45.130
- If there were a president
who you didn't like,

01:29:45.130 --> 01:29:48.110
but asked you to be his
or her science advisor,

01:29:48.110 --> 01:29:49.600
what would you say?

01:29:49.600 --> 01:29:51.060
- Well, it would depend on the president.

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:53.840
- Why? If you're asked to advise them,

01:29:53.840 --> 01:29:55.270
why should it depend on the president.

01:29:55.270 --> 01:29:56.103
It depends on you.

01:29:56.103 --> 01:30:00.750
- No, you have to believe that
the president will listen.

01:30:00.750 --> 01:30:01.683
You have to believe--

01:30:01.683 --> 01:30:05.059
- Wait, wait, a minute, if
you're not an advisor at all,

01:30:05.059 --> 01:30:07.773
if you're not even in the room,

01:30:08.620 --> 01:30:09.990
they're clearly not listening to you

01:30:09.990 --> 01:30:10.940
because you're not in the room.

01:30:10.940 --> 01:30:11.773
- No, that's true.

01:30:11.773 --> 01:30:13.760
- So then, there's a chance
they'll listen to you

01:30:13.760 --> 01:30:14.593
if you're in the room.

01:30:14.593 --> 01:30:15.800
- You have to figure out whether you're

01:30:15.800 --> 01:30:19.210
gonna be more effective advising
this particular president,

01:30:19.210 --> 01:30:22.720
or more effective pursuing
the same issues from outside.

01:30:22.720 --> 01:30:23.553
- From outside.

01:30:23.553 --> 01:30:24.480
- You have to make that decision.

01:30:24.480 --> 01:30:27.146
- It can't always work to
yell science into a wig.

01:30:27.146 --> 01:30:29.500
(laughter)

01:30:29.500 --> 01:30:30.660
- Can I just say something
about these two doctors

01:30:30.660 --> 01:30:33.630
that's extraordinary and
people should recognize this.

01:30:33.630 --> 01:30:35.000
The whole idea of our country

01:30:35.000 --> 01:30:36.370
in The Declaration of Independence,

01:30:36.370 --> 01:30:39.280
which, this genius document
that frankly had flaws,

01:30:39.280 --> 01:30:42.170
it referred to Native Americans as savages

01:30:42.170 --> 01:30:45.540
and all the flaws of the genius
of the writers at the time

01:30:45.540 --> 01:30:48.770
that they had flaws. But they
kind of came to a conclusion

01:30:48.770 --> 01:30:51.100
at the end, where they
basically said for this country,

01:30:51.100 --> 01:30:53.170
the idea of this nation,
which was not founded

01:30:53.170 --> 01:30:56.030
like other countries 'cuz we
all look alike or pray alike

01:30:56.030 --> 01:30:57.258
or are descended in the same way.

01:30:57.258 --> 01:30:59.700
The idea of this country
was the first nation

01:30:59.700 --> 01:31:03.150
of ideas and principles.
And especially then,

01:31:03.150 --> 01:31:05.060
it was a tenuous way of forming a nation.

01:31:05.060 --> 01:31:07.650
And so what these two
doctors really respresent

01:31:07.650 --> 01:31:10.790
to me, is what our founders
said is gonna have to happen.

01:31:10.790 --> 01:31:13.350
If this country's gonna make
it, they basically said,

01:31:13.350 --> 01:31:16.460
we have to have an unusual
commitment to each other,

01:31:16.460 --> 01:31:18.581
that goes beyond just
tolerating each other,

01:31:18.581 --> 01:31:21.660
or kind of admiring each
other, they basically said

01:31:21.660 --> 01:31:23.503
we have to commit to each other.

01:31:24.440 --> 01:31:25.273
And this is the final words

01:31:25.273 --> 01:31:26.447
of The Declaration of Independence,

01:31:26.447 --> 01:31:30.267
"We must mutually pledge
to each other our lives,

01:31:30.267 --> 01:31:33.430
"our fortunes, and our sacred honor."

01:31:33.430 --> 01:31:35.390
And it's something we
all should think about.

01:31:35.390 --> 01:31:37.120
Are we living our lives this way?

01:31:37.120 --> 01:31:39.970
These folks, and they were
very humble, but trust me,

01:31:39.970 --> 01:31:42.810
they could've probably
made a lot more money,

01:31:42.810 --> 01:31:43.643
a lot more resources--

01:31:43.643 --> 01:31:45.230
- You don't get rich
working in government.

01:31:45.230 --> 01:31:47.360
- You do not get rich. But
these are folks that said,

01:31:47.360 --> 01:31:51.560
you know what, my love of
this country, my sacred honor,

01:31:51.560 --> 01:31:54.120
my commitment of my fortune, will be

01:31:54.120 --> 01:31:55.670
to this nation's goodness.

01:31:55.670 --> 01:31:59.170
And it's been those folks, who
are those irrational people

01:31:59.170 --> 01:32:00.640
throughout history, who
may not even make it

01:32:00.640 --> 01:32:03.150
into the history books,
but have had consistently

01:32:03.150 --> 01:32:05.030
had this commitment.
Those people that built

01:32:05.030 --> 01:32:07.210
the greatest infrastructure
this nation has ever known,

01:32:07.210 --> 01:32:09.930
most of whose names aren't
known, The Underground Railroad.

01:32:09.930 --> 01:32:12.350
Those people who, I can't name the people,

01:32:12.350 --> 01:32:14.440
who whether they stormed
beaches in Normandy

01:32:14.440 --> 01:32:18.460
or sat in laboratories
designing technologies,

01:32:18.460 --> 01:32:21.730
innovations that I use every
day, but I take for granted.

01:32:21.730 --> 01:32:23.780
And so I just wanna thank them publicly,

01:32:23.780 --> 01:32:25.670
because they don't often
get a moment like this,

01:32:25.670 --> 01:32:26.940
before a huge audience.

01:32:26.940 --> 01:32:27.773
- Hardly ever.

01:32:27.773 --> 01:32:29.320
- Hardly ever, to get
the kind of gratitude

01:32:29.320 --> 01:32:30.399
and celebration they deserve.

01:32:30.399 --> 01:32:34.810
Thank you very much.
(applause)

01:32:34.810 --> 01:32:38.140
- Let's bring up the house
lights, and we'll take some Q&amp;A,

01:32:38.140 --> 01:32:40.370
we'll do this for like 20 minutes.

01:32:40.370 --> 01:32:41.630
We got a few minutes.

01:32:41.630 --> 01:32:43.140
I don't know, do we have microphones?

01:32:43.140 --> 01:32:45.410
There's a microphone on the aisle there.

01:32:45.410 --> 01:32:47.620
And we have another
microphone on the aisle there.

01:32:47.620 --> 01:32:49.370
Okay, so who's the first person up?

01:32:50.470 --> 01:32:53.133
Right there, okay. Go ahead.

01:32:55.720 --> 01:32:58.321
Wait, wait, you turn that on. Hang on.

01:32:58.321 --> 01:33:01.404
(indistinct chatter)

01:33:02.920 --> 01:33:04.420
Yeah, we got it now. Go ahead.

01:33:05.813 --> 01:33:07.307
- [Audience Member] We
know, how black holes--

01:33:07.307 --> 01:33:09.858
- Just wait wait, how old are you?

01:33:09.858 --> 01:33:12.048
- [Audience Member] Uh, 6.

01:33:12.048 --> 01:33:13.000
- Wait, how old are you?

01:33:13.000 --> 01:33:14.100
- [Audience Member] 6.

01:33:14.100 --> 01:33:15.273
- You're 6?

01:33:15.273 --> 01:33:19.523
- [Audience Member] Yes.
(applause)

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:23.960
- Well, welcome to StarTalk.
I hope we didn't use

01:33:23.960 --> 01:33:25.803
too much grown up language here.

01:33:26.770 --> 01:33:29.063
Yeah, sometimes we do. I apologize.

01:33:30.164 --> 01:33:31.465
But I bet we didn't use any language

01:33:31.465 --> 01:33:34.250
that you'd never heard
your parents use, you see?

01:33:34.250 --> 01:33:35.630
So I bet.

01:33:35.630 --> 01:33:37.300
So what is your question?

01:33:37.300 --> 01:33:39.393
Hold the microphone right
in front of your mouth.

01:33:40.270 --> 01:33:42.580
- [Audience Member] We
know how black holes form

01:33:42.580 --> 01:33:47.580
to supernovas, how do super
massive black holes form?

01:33:48.635 --> 01:33:49.566
- Oh, yeah.

01:33:49.566 --> 01:33:52.899
(applause and laughter)

01:33:55.633 --> 01:33:57.983
- It's a little off topic, kid.

01:33:57.983 --> 01:34:00.233
(laughter)

01:34:01.710 --> 01:34:03.332
- Senator, can you answer that for me?

01:34:03.332 --> 01:34:05.910
(laughter)

01:34:05.910 --> 01:34:09.530
So, high mass stars, when they die,

01:34:09.530 --> 01:34:12.230
they leave behind a
black hole as a remnant.

01:34:12.230 --> 01:34:13.220
You wanna avoid that.

01:34:13.220 --> 01:34:17.260
But there'll be many of
those across the galaxy,

01:34:17.260 --> 01:34:19.940
but there are black holes
that are much more massive

01:34:19.940 --> 01:34:24.170
than that, one per galaxy
in each galaxy center.

01:34:24.170 --> 01:34:27.000
And those we call super
massive black holes.

01:34:27.000 --> 01:34:29.893
And we don't really have a
good idea of how they form.

01:34:29.893 --> 01:34:30.726
(laughter)

01:34:30.726 --> 01:34:33.419
- So, I can't help you.

01:34:33.419 --> 01:34:38.247
But, if you are asking that question now,

01:34:39.380 --> 01:34:41.260
here's the challenge.

01:34:41.260 --> 01:34:44.580
When we are born, the parents
spend the first few years

01:34:44.580 --> 01:34:46.500
teaching us to walk and talk,

01:34:46.500 --> 01:34:48.130
and then they spend the rest of our lives

01:34:48.130 --> 01:34:50.620
telling us to shut up and sit down.

01:34:50.620 --> 01:34:53.090
Clearly, in your case, your curiosity

01:34:53.090 --> 01:34:54.400
is burning within you.

01:34:54.400 --> 01:34:56.510
If you carry that
curiosity into adulthood,

01:34:56.510 --> 01:34:59.000
you may be the first person to discover

01:34:59.000 --> 01:35:00.767
how super massive black holes occur.

01:35:00.767 --> 01:35:03.017
(applause)

01:35:04.820 --> 01:35:05.970
- Right over here, yes.

01:35:09.942 --> 01:35:12.730
- [Audience Member] Hello
Dr. Tyson, Senator Booker,

01:35:12.730 --> 01:35:15.776
everyone there on the panel.
Thank you very much for coming.

01:35:15.776 --> 01:35:18.750
Well firstly let me say,
Dr. Tyson, you said that

01:35:18.750 --> 01:35:20.153
you don't believe in hope,
and I wanna say something

01:35:20.153 --> 01:35:22.130
that we've probably all heard

01:35:22.130 --> 01:35:24.670
since before Christmas,
is that revolutions

01:35:24.670 --> 01:35:29.670
are built on hope. So also, I
wanted to ask Senator Booker,

01:35:30.380 --> 01:35:32.070
you had mentioned about
the Superfund sites

01:35:32.070 --> 01:35:34.280
that we have throughout New Jersey,

01:35:34.280 --> 01:35:37.550
and I wanted to also discuss with you,

01:35:37.550 --> 01:35:39.160
since this is the Garden State and we like

01:35:39.160 --> 01:35:40.540
to do a lot more of our own gardening

01:35:40.540 --> 01:35:42.270
and you had mentioned
trying to return more

01:35:42.270 --> 01:35:44.253
to a plant-based diet. Many of the things

01:35:44.253 --> 01:35:48.160
that we use in our own yards are not good

01:35:48.160 --> 01:35:49.950
for the environment, they're
not good for the food

01:35:49.950 --> 01:35:51.253
that we're eating, and they're
not good for the things

01:35:51.253 --> 01:35:53.310
that we use in our own gardens.

01:35:53.310 --> 01:35:55.690
And what are you doing, as a senator,

01:35:55.690 --> 01:35:59.070
to help us bring more ecologically sound

01:35:59.070 --> 01:36:00.840
and more environmentally sound things

01:36:00.840 --> 01:36:04.260
to our own gardens, that
are not being, you know,

01:36:04.260 --> 01:36:06.830
pushed as they should be.

01:36:06.830 --> 01:36:08.937
- That's a great question.
We can broaden that to say

01:36:08.937 --> 01:36:12.440
what kind of things can we
and should we do locally

01:36:12.440 --> 01:36:13.760
that will matter globally?

01:36:13.760 --> 01:36:16.210
Is that a fair extension of that question?

01:36:16.210 --> 01:36:17.700
- Sure. So I just want everybody to know

01:36:17.700 --> 01:36:21.200
that there's a Farm Bill
reauthorization coming up.

01:36:21.200 --> 01:36:22.033
- Yeah!

01:36:22.033 --> 01:36:22.990
- It was days when I came into the Senate

01:36:22.990 --> 01:36:25.970
that I voted against it,
because of a lot of reasons.

01:36:25.970 --> 01:36:28.700
One, it made a massive cut to food stamps,

01:36:28.700 --> 01:36:32.610
but also because they
gave massive subsidization

01:36:32.610 --> 01:36:34.958
to these massive corporations who engage

01:36:34.958 --> 01:36:38.470
in farming practices that
are polluting the soil,

01:36:38.470 --> 01:36:42.290
polluting our rivers,
and subsidizing them with

01:36:42.290 --> 01:36:44.640
our tax dollars,
subsidizing things that we

01:36:44.640 --> 01:36:47.170
tell Americans not to eat.
Our government on one hand

01:36:47.170 --> 01:36:48.670
tells you not to eat this stuff,

01:36:48.670 --> 01:36:50.490
and then we're subsidizing
on the other hand,

01:36:50.490 --> 01:36:52.560
so that when kids in,
now I'm going back home

01:36:52.560 --> 01:36:55.210
to Newark tonight, when kids
in Newark walk into a bodega,

01:36:55.210 --> 01:36:58.130
and see a Twinkie product
cheaper than an apple,

01:36:58.130 --> 01:37:00.920
it's because we're subsidizing that stuff.

01:37:00.920 --> 01:37:04.140
And the reality is, then
those kids are getting

01:37:04.140 --> 01:37:06.890
Type II Diabetes as children, and we have

01:37:06.890 --> 01:37:08.610
to subsidize that health care.

01:37:08.610 --> 01:37:11.180
So what I'm saying is,
this food system we have

01:37:11.180 --> 01:37:12.960
needs a lot of repair.

01:37:12.960 --> 01:37:14.795
So what I believe and exciting me

01:37:14.795 --> 01:37:15.640
that you're asking this question,

01:37:15.640 --> 01:37:18.270
I started when I was
mayor is, I love this idea

01:37:18.270 --> 01:37:20.860
of locally grown, locally sourced things.

01:37:20.860 --> 01:37:24.050
And we should be encouraging
that kind of agriculture

01:37:24.050 --> 01:37:26.780
in our communities. The
Farm Bill actually got

01:37:26.780 --> 01:37:28.460
some stuff in there I think that was good

01:37:28.460 --> 01:37:31.300
about locally grown, organic stuff,

01:37:31.300 --> 01:37:32.290
but I think more of it should be.

01:37:32.290 --> 01:37:34.240
So the end of the answer, I would say is

01:37:34.240 --> 01:37:36.300
find out what's happening
with this coming big

01:37:36.300 --> 01:37:38.620
Farm reauthorization
bill. Let's work together.

01:37:38.620 --> 01:37:41.300
You're one of my constituents,
reach out to my office.

01:37:41.300 --> 01:37:43.460
I have a group of people
that are very passionate

01:37:43.460 --> 01:37:46.000
on my staff about this Farm
Bill and what we can be doing

01:37:46.000 --> 01:37:48.900
to support the kind of local
farming you're talking about.

01:37:48.900 --> 01:37:52.087
- And New Jersey is one of
the leaders in local farming

01:37:52.087 --> 01:37:53.987
and urban farming.
- I love that you know that.

01:37:53.987 --> 01:37:56.840
Yeah, in local farming,
we're a farm state.

01:37:56.840 --> 01:37:59.330
And urban farming, in fact
when I was mayor of Newark,

01:37:59.330 --> 01:38:00.729
we created Newark as the largest--

01:38:00.729 --> 01:38:02.800
- Wait that's a thing, urban farming?

01:38:02.800 --> 01:38:03.970
- Oh, absolutely.
- That's a thing?

01:38:03.970 --> 01:38:06.960
- Acres and acres, it's a good
way to banish food deserts

01:38:06.960 --> 01:38:09.450
and exciting stuff is
happening in urban farming.

01:38:09.450 --> 01:38:11.950
Roof gardens, there's a
company in New Jersey called

01:38:11.950 --> 01:38:15.200
AeroFarms, which uses very
little, vertical farming.

01:38:15.200 --> 01:38:17.160
All these innovations, less water,

01:38:17.160 --> 01:38:18.890
it's really exciting stuff.

01:38:18.890 --> 01:38:20.990
- Let's see if we can answer
our questions a little quickly,

01:38:20.990 --> 01:38:23.930
because we have a thousand
trillion people online.

01:38:23.930 --> 01:38:24.883
Yes, right here.

01:38:26.520 --> 01:38:28.220
- [Audience Member] This
time it's for everyone

01:38:28.220 --> 01:38:30.300
but especially Jo, as a female scientist.

01:38:30.300 --> 01:38:35.300
I have three daughters
ages 12, 10, and almost 4.

01:38:35.670 --> 01:38:38.530
And they start off really
inquisitive and the 6 year old,

01:38:38.530 --> 01:38:40.180
and they wanna know about black holes

01:38:40.180 --> 01:38:43.100
and my daughter, my oldest,
is in middle school now.

01:38:43.100 --> 01:38:46.320
And she has won in her elementary
school her science fair.

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:48.180
And she thought it was the coolest thing,

01:38:48.180 --> 01:38:50.420
and was still into science,
and now in middle school,

01:38:50.420 --> 01:38:53.140
you can see that tapering off.

01:38:53.140 --> 01:38:56.710
And the disinterest,
for females especially,

01:38:56.710 --> 01:38:59.930
but for her in science.
And how do you propose

01:38:59.930 --> 01:39:02.717
that we keep that
curiosity, as you have said,

01:39:02.717 --> 01:39:04.210
and especially for women?

01:39:04.210 --> 01:39:07.356
Because I work in a job
who doesn't have a lot

01:39:07.356 --> 01:39:10.080
of women either, but that's construction.

01:39:10.080 --> 01:39:13.470
How do we keep the women
wanting to do science

01:39:13.470 --> 01:39:16.280
when they're not encouraged to do so?

01:39:16.280 --> 01:39:17.927
- Well, I think that's a great question.

01:39:17.927 --> 01:39:20.860
And we can all play a role
in that by encouraging

01:39:20.860 --> 01:39:23.620
girls to do it, and
showing them role models.

01:39:23.620 --> 01:39:25.470
One of the reasons that girls turn away

01:39:25.470 --> 01:39:27.650
from science in middle
school is they're starting

01:39:27.650 --> 01:39:29.660
to come to grips with being women,

01:39:29.660 --> 01:39:32.700
and they think well I have
to be a woman or a scientist,

01:39:32.700 --> 01:39:34.684
because they often don't see examples

01:39:34.684 --> 01:39:38.300
of normal, healthy, exciting
women that run marathons

01:39:38.300 --> 01:39:42.610
or have kids or do whatever
it is that women do.

01:39:42.610 --> 01:39:45.530
And so, one of the things we
worked on in the White House

01:39:45.530 --> 01:39:49.020
and that I continue
currently is how do we change

01:39:49.020 --> 01:39:52.720
the image of science and
technology in Hollywood?

01:39:52.720 --> 01:39:54.830
And I think they can play an enormous role

01:39:54.830 --> 01:39:58.570
in inspiring our girls and
minorities to be scientists,

01:39:58.570 --> 01:40:01.820
just by showing the whole
breadth of scientists

01:40:01.820 --> 01:40:04.920
and the jobs that they do.
So it's a great question.

01:40:04.920 --> 01:40:07.590
On a small scale, we
could just encourage them

01:40:07.590 --> 01:40:09.596
and show them examples.

01:40:09.596 --> 01:40:12.240
(applause)

01:40:12.240 --> 01:40:13.130
- Yes, over here.

01:40:14.107 --> 01:40:16.050
- [Audience Member]
Hello, my name is Emily.

01:40:16.050 --> 01:40:17.260
I've spoken to you once before,

01:40:17.260 --> 01:40:18.957
you probably don't remember me.

01:40:18.957 --> 01:40:20.720
(laughter)

01:40:20.720 --> 01:40:23.900
But I know this is slightly off topic,

01:40:23.900 --> 01:40:26.213
I was wondering if I
could have your autograph?

01:40:26.213 --> 01:40:29.720
(laughter)
- Is she talking to you?

01:40:29.720 --> 01:40:31.516
- No, she's talking to you.

01:40:31.516 --> 01:40:33.943
- Oh, my autograph. Oh.

01:40:36.011 --> 01:40:38.261
(cheering)

01:40:39.584 --> 01:40:41.170
If you leave something with an usher,

01:40:41.170 --> 01:40:42.820
I'd be happy to do it after the show.

01:40:42.820 --> 01:40:43.653
- All right.

01:40:43.653 --> 01:40:45.140
- I'd be happy to do it. And
you're wearing a NASA shirt?

01:40:45.140 --> 01:40:45.973
Or does that say something else on it?

01:40:45.973 --> 01:40:48.966
- It's a rogue NASA shirt,
it says rogue on it.

01:40:48.966 --> 01:40:50.440
- Cool.

01:40:50.440 --> 01:40:55.010
Yeah it's in the spirit
of what we're doing.

01:40:55.010 --> 01:40:56.627
So give something for
me to sign to an usher

01:40:56.627 --> 01:40:57.762
and I'd be happy to do so.

01:40:57.762 --> 01:40:58.595
- All right.

01:40:58.595 --> 01:40:59.428
- Okay.

01:40:59.428 --> 01:41:01.020
- Am I allowed to ask a question?

01:41:01.020 --> 01:41:02.570
- Did you also have a question?

01:41:02.570 --> 01:41:03.887
- Um yes, really quick if I could ask.

01:41:03.887 --> 01:41:05.220
- Try to keep the questions quick

01:41:05.220 --> 01:41:06.649
so we can get through the line, go.

01:41:06.649 --> 01:41:07.770
- If I could ask Senator Booker--

01:41:07.770 --> 01:41:08.603
- Yes.

01:41:08.603 --> 01:41:10.220
- I was just thinking,
I've talked with my parents

01:41:10.220 --> 01:41:14.067
about this before. For like
the younger generation,

01:41:14.067 --> 01:41:17.320
what could we possibly do
besides just protesting.

01:41:17.320 --> 01:41:19.500
Like I've been to a
Women's March, but like

01:41:19.500 --> 01:41:21.560
is there anything else
that the younger generation

01:41:21.560 --> 01:41:24.410
can do since we're
basically what's going to be

01:41:24.410 --> 01:41:28.350
the future of politics and science.

01:41:28.350 --> 01:41:29.183
- Can I?

01:41:29.183 --> 01:41:30.210
- Go ahead.

01:41:30.210 --> 01:41:31.673
- No, can I add to that?

01:41:32.850 --> 01:41:35.830
Why is it that the most effective protests

01:41:35.830 --> 01:41:37.910
are only the ones that turn violent,

01:41:37.910 --> 01:41:39.990
'cuz then they make more
headlines across the country?

01:41:39.990 --> 01:41:41.530
- I disagree completely
with that statement.

01:41:41.530 --> 01:41:42.740
- I do too.

01:41:42.740 --> 01:41:44.650
- The most effective protests are the ones

01:41:44.650 --> 01:41:46.750
that forge connections
between those who show up

01:41:46.750 --> 01:41:48.720
and ones that change the narrative of how

01:41:48.720 --> 01:41:50.760
the media covers an issue.
They're the ones that

01:41:50.760 --> 01:41:52.920
encourage people to follow
up with that protest

01:41:52.920 --> 01:41:55.780
by voting, by running
for office themselves,

01:41:55.780 --> 01:41:57.800
by joining a local group that is embedded

01:41:57.800 --> 01:42:00.090
in their community. So the
violence is a spectacle,

01:42:00.090 --> 01:42:01.680
it's often a distraction forged by people

01:42:01.680 --> 01:42:03.030
who don't wanna see change happen.

01:42:03.030 --> 01:42:05.140
That is not an effective
protest, that's a failure,

01:42:05.140 --> 01:42:06.990
and it's disruption by
those who oppose it.

01:42:06.990 --> 01:42:10.200
For young people who wanna
do stuff besides protest,

01:42:10.200 --> 01:42:13.440
yo just be curious like Neil said.

01:42:13.440 --> 01:42:17.610
Run for something. Represent
your people somewhere.

01:42:17.610 --> 01:42:19.490
Whether it's, like, your school board

01:42:19.490 --> 01:42:22.560
within your school itself,
if you have the opportunity

01:42:22.560 --> 01:42:25.840
to represent others, that's
giving to other people.

01:42:25.840 --> 01:42:28.830
And you're living what
you're asking others to do.

01:42:28.830 --> 01:42:31.010
And that's the best way to kinda change

01:42:31.010 --> 01:42:33.420
the world around you. Stop
asking others to do it,

01:42:33.420 --> 01:42:35.270
and do what the Senator
said, do it yourself

01:42:35.270 --> 01:42:37.778
at the level that you can
achieve. That's the beginning.

01:42:37.778 --> 01:42:40.629
(applause)

01:42:40.629 --> 01:42:41.994
- I got you man, I got you.

01:42:41.994 --> 01:42:43.380
(laughter)

01:42:43.380 --> 01:42:46.190
- The protege. Okay quick,
quick questions please.

01:42:46.190 --> 01:42:47.990
- [Audience Member] So I've
been teaching public school

01:42:47.990 --> 01:42:48.823
for 8 years.

01:42:48.823 --> 01:42:51.443
- Whoa, public school teacher, give it up.

01:42:51.443 --> 01:42:53.940
(applause)

01:42:53.940 --> 01:42:56.100
- [Audience Member] Are
my students' brains,

01:42:56.100 --> 01:42:57.610
this is kind of abstract,

01:42:57.610 --> 01:43:00.955
