01:43:47.517 --> 01:43:49.950
- [Audience Member] I'm glad you do.

01:43:49.950 --> 01:43:52.527
I work at the Motion Center
for Independent Living

01:43:52.527 --> 01:43:56.667
and I'm wondering what policy and science

01:43:59.410 --> 01:44:02.484
can do for the welfare of
people living with disabilities.

01:44:02.484 --> 01:44:03.317
- Wow.

01:44:03.317 --> 01:44:04.150
- Ooh.

01:44:04.150 --> 01:44:06.400
(applause)

01:44:07.400 --> 01:44:09.520
- This is a broader health issue. Correct?

01:44:09.520 --> 01:44:11.370
- Yeah there's exciting things for--

01:44:11.370 --> 01:44:13.190
- I think there are really exciting things

01:44:13.190 --> 01:44:17.990
coming forward using
technology to compensate

01:44:17.990 --> 01:44:19.280
for what people can't do,

01:44:19.280 --> 01:44:22.400
either because of age or disability.

01:44:22.400 --> 01:44:24.890
And I think that technology
is really going to be

01:44:24.890 --> 01:44:26.940
developing fast in the next few years,

01:44:26.940 --> 01:44:29.370
because the demand for
it will be increasing

01:44:29.370 --> 01:44:34.250
as our citizenry ages. So I
think it's a really great time

01:44:34.250 --> 01:44:37.280
for those kinds of questions.
But I think we need to

01:44:37.280 --> 01:44:40.020
look at how people actually
live. And there are some

01:44:40.020 --> 01:44:43.640
studies that use virtual reality to study

01:44:43.640 --> 01:44:46.320
how people actually live in their homes,

01:44:46.320 --> 01:44:50.580
or wherever they live, and
then develop technology

01:44:50.580 --> 01:44:53.010
around that. And I think
that's much more accurate

01:44:53.010 --> 01:44:55.170
than trying to imagine how people live.

01:44:55.170 --> 01:44:57.590
Which is unfortunately,
how a lot of that research

01:44:57.590 --> 01:44:59.900
has been done in the past.
A lot of assumptions made.

01:44:59.900 --> 01:45:01.060
- Plus, there'll be a difference

01:45:01.060 --> 01:45:03.660
between supporting someone
who has a disability,

01:45:03.660 --> 01:45:05.772
and then repairing the
disability outright.

01:45:05.772 --> 01:45:06.605
- Right.

01:45:06.605 --> 01:45:08.210
- Which in principle,
could happen one day.

01:45:08.210 --> 01:45:10.960
- Yes.
- And then we all live a life

01:45:10.960 --> 01:45:14.770
we live active lives, until we die.

01:45:14.770 --> 01:45:16.710
Rather than active lives until we get

01:45:16.710 --> 01:45:19.588
infirmed, and then stay
infirmed until we die.

01:45:19.588 --> 01:45:20.421
- Right.

01:45:20.421 --> 01:45:22.950
- I might add that it
won't be too long before

01:45:22.950 --> 01:45:27.230
we're 3D printing new organs,
so that the organ wait list

01:45:27.230 --> 01:45:30.303
that now exists for transplanted organs

01:45:30.303 --> 01:45:32.280
will become a thing of the past,

01:45:32.280 --> 01:45:35.700
because we can 3D print new organs.

01:45:35.700 --> 01:45:36.985
The second thing of note, for nerds--

01:45:36.985 --> 01:45:37.818
- What?

01:45:37.818 --> 01:45:38.651
(laughter)

01:45:38.651 --> 01:45:39.484
- Yep.

01:45:40.526 --> 01:45:41.442
- Okay, that's cool.

01:45:41.442 --> 01:45:42.700
- That's what's happening.

01:45:42.700 --> 01:45:44.460
- Your printer at home, is
a different kinda printer.

01:45:44.460 --> 01:45:45.730
- Oh, it's different?

01:45:45.730 --> 01:45:46.563
- But it's happening.

01:45:46.563 --> 01:45:48.050
- I was gonna say, mine
doesn't take blood.

01:45:48.050 --> 01:45:49.902
- It takes more toner than usual.

01:45:49.902 --> 01:45:50.900
(laughter)

01:45:50.900 --> 01:45:53.960
- And for the nerds out
there who are interested

01:45:53.960 --> 01:45:56.870
in this particular question,
the president's council

01:45:56.870 --> 01:45:59.744
of advisors on science and
technology did a report

01:45:59.744 --> 01:46:04.620
in the past year on technology
to assist with aging.

01:46:04.620 --> 01:46:05.877
Which doesn't address all of the question

01:46:05.877 --> 01:46:08.697
the questioner had in mind,
but it addresses some of them.

01:46:08.697 --> 01:46:12.020
And that report is online
in the Obama archive.

01:46:12.020 --> 01:46:13.113
- Okay, cool.

01:46:15.700 --> 01:46:16.533
(applause)

01:46:16.533 --> 01:46:18.880
Let's take another one from up top, yes.

01:46:18.880 --> 01:46:20.430
