01:53:37.019 --> 01:53:38.510
Because they're black.
(laughter)

01:53:38.510 --> 01:53:39.343
- That's racist.

01:53:39.343 --> 01:53:41.358
- Oh that's racist?

01:53:41.358 --> 01:53:44.444
- I don't like this one
bit, that is not cool.

01:53:44.444 --> 01:53:46.694
(laughter)

01:53:47.810 --> 01:53:51.070
- But black holes can render
their existence visible

01:53:51.070 --> 01:53:52.660
by indirect means.

01:53:52.660 --> 01:53:56.520
For example, in the flaying
of star, whose outer layers

01:53:56.520 --> 01:54:00.550
are spiraling down within
and then that toilet bowl

01:54:00.550 --> 01:54:05.550
style sucking action radiates copiously

01:54:06.230 --> 01:54:08.740
in ultraviolet and x-rays.
And use an ultraviolet

01:54:08.740 --> 01:54:11.230
telescope and an x-ray
telescope, and they pick 'em out

01:54:11.230 --> 01:54:13.340
all across the galaxy.

01:54:13.340 --> 01:54:15.820
So, it's just a testament
to the methods and tools

01:54:15.820 --> 01:54:18.940
of science that demonstrate
that the five senses

01:54:18.940 --> 01:54:20.930
that we are biologically endowed with

01:54:20.930 --> 01:54:22.930
are wholly inadequate to decode

01:54:22.930 --> 01:54:25.110
the actual operations of nature.

01:54:25.110 --> 01:54:27.550
And so, that's what telescopes do for us.

01:54:27.550 --> 01:54:30.780
But otherwise, I'm not up on
the Event Horizon Telescope.

01:54:30.780 --> 01:54:32.830
I'm embarrassed, I should know.

01:54:32.830 --> 01:54:36.100
But I like to be candid in my ignorance.

01:54:36.100 --> 01:54:37.293
So, there it is.

01:54:38.580 --> 01:54:40.190
- Thanks for not trying to pass laws based

01:54:40.190 --> 01:54:41.400
on that ignorance, I appreciate that.

01:54:41.400 --> 01:54:43.900
- Oh, yeah. If you're ignorant, yeah,

01:54:43.900 --> 01:54:45.930
you should just be self aware of that.

01:54:45.930 --> 01:54:49.043
And not try to create
legislation based on it.

01:54:49.043 --> 01:54:49.876
(laughter)

01:54:49.876 --> 01:54:51.363
Let's go up top, one more time.

01:54:52.290 --> 01:54:53.380
- [Audience Member] Hi. Oh wow.

01:54:53.380 --> 01:54:55.527
I'm a high school senior and next year

01:54:55.527 --> 01:54:57.390
I'm going to school to study physics,

01:54:57.390 --> 01:54:59.550
in a large part due to you and the things

01:54:59.550 --> 01:55:01.765
that you put out there,

01:55:01.765 --> 01:55:04.260
so thank you.
- Oh, well thank you.

01:55:04.260 --> 01:55:07.070
- My question's for
you and Senator Booker.

01:55:07.070 --> 01:55:11.197
Someone once told me,
you can't reason someone

01:55:11.197 --> 01:55:13.913
out of something they didn't
reason themselves into.

01:55:15.010 --> 01:55:16.493
How do you fight that,

01:55:18.203 --> 01:55:21.860
whether it's evolution or
public policy or whatever.

01:55:21.860 --> 01:55:25.220
- Yeah, I think that
statement is like 85% true.

01:55:25.220 --> 01:55:29.180
And so, I think it implies

01:55:29.180 --> 01:55:31.540
that winning an argument involves reason.

01:55:31.540 --> 01:55:35.090
But winning an argument
can involve demonstration.

01:55:35.090 --> 01:55:38.900
Right? If someone doesn't
think there's global warming,

01:55:38.900 --> 01:55:42.910
you just invite them to buy
property on the ocean's edge.

01:55:42.910 --> 01:55:44.101
And then you step back.

01:55:44.101 --> 01:55:46.351
(laughter)

01:55:47.880 --> 01:55:49.940
They convert very quickly,
and you didn't have

01:55:49.940 --> 01:55:51.900
to lay down a single argument.

01:55:51.900 --> 01:55:55.000
So I think there are
ways of convincing people

01:55:55.000 --> 01:55:57.570
that don't involve logical arguments.

01:55:57.570 --> 01:56:02.400
And we need to be more
savvy about what method

01:56:02.400 --> 01:56:05.900
might be best invoked given the
person, given the situation.

01:56:05.900 --> 01:56:09.380
And I, as an educator,
invest a stupid amount

01:56:09.380 --> 01:56:12.540
of energy, mental energy, thinking about

01:56:14.939 --> 01:56:17.148
what are the thought pathways

01:56:17.148 --> 01:56:21.000
that occupy the person who
I'm communicating with?

01:56:21.000 --> 01:56:24.330
And I then align what I'm trying to say

01:56:24.330 --> 01:56:28.470
with those thought pathways,
so that I can maximally

01:56:30.920 --> 01:56:34.350
send information into you on a level

01:56:34.350 --> 01:56:35.890
where you can take ownership of it.

01:56:35.890 --> 01:56:37.867
And you can say, "Wow, I
never knew it that way.

01:56:37.867 --> 01:56:40.790
"Oh my gosh, I figured it
out. I understand it now."

01:56:40.790 --> 01:56:44.040
That takes a key, and
sometimes there's a different

01:56:44.040 --> 01:56:45.750
key for every single person.

01:56:45.750 --> 01:56:49.020
And that's hard. That's a
higher level of expectation

01:56:49.020 --> 01:56:51.280
from an educator than
just lecturing against

01:56:51.280 --> 01:56:53.900
a chalkboard hoping
that you walk up behind

01:56:53.900 --> 01:56:56.420
and pick it up and
understand what's going on.

01:56:56.420 --> 01:56:57.897
- A piece of advice, real quick from me,

01:56:57.897 --> 01:56:59.870
and I learned this the
hard way when I was young.

01:56:59.870 --> 01:57:01.220
I finally had this aha moment.

01:57:01.220 --> 01:57:03.290
So this is simply the piece of advice.

01:57:03.290 --> 01:57:06.240
You do not have to attend every argument

01:57:06.240 --> 01:57:07.972
that you are invited to.

01:57:07.972 --> 01:57:11.305
(laughter and applause)

01:57:14.140 --> 01:57:15.650
- We've got another, a person here.

01:57:15.650 --> 01:57:16.560
By the way, you have one of the

01:57:16.560 --> 01:57:20.222
very few official shirts
issued by StarTalk.

01:57:20.222 --> 01:57:23.663
Yes, go on, a question.
Get close to the mic.

01:57:24.560 --> 01:57:25.850
- [Audience Member] I, as
a middle school student,

01:57:25.850 --> 01:57:27.880
and many other middle
school students out there

01:57:27.880 --> 01:57:30.930
are being told to sit down
and shut up more than ever.

01:57:30.930 --> 01:57:31.820
Why is that?

01:57:31.820 --> 01:57:34.053
And what could we do about it?

01:57:35.670 --> 01:57:37.122
- Stand up and shout.

01:57:37.122 --> 01:57:39.300
(laughter)

01:57:39.300 --> 01:57:41.690
- Yeah, so you're in middle school now?

01:57:41.690 --> 01:57:43.660
- Yes, I am. In eighth
grade. So I'm gonna be

01:57:43.660 --> 01:57:45.027
going into high school soon.

01:57:45.027 --> 01:57:47.090
- Yeah, yeah. So your teachers are saying,

01:57:47.090 --> 01:57:50.060
the good students are the
ones that are quiet and obey.

01:57:50.060 --> 01:57:51.300
- [Audience Member] Yeah, to a point.

01:57:51.300 --> 01:57:54.820
Some allow questions,
some don't, to an extent.

01:57:54.820 --> 01:57:56.670
- Yeah. So one of the challenges

01:57:56.670 --> 01:58:01.455
to the middle school
teacher is that you all are

01:58:01.455 --> 01:58:04.842
hormonially crazy at this point.

01:58:04.842 --> 01:58:07.092
(laughter)

01:58:07.975 --> 01:58:10.250
(Neil stutters)

01:58:10.250 --> 01:58:13.562
Hormonally out of control.
- You're like a screw worm.

01:58:13.562 --> 01:58:15.812
(laughter)

01:58:18.301 --> 01:58:19.301
- True that.

01:58:21.220 --> 01:58:24.930
- You know, I can't go in there
and like slap the teachers.

01:58:24.930 --> 01:58:29.930
But if you know that your
curiosity and your energy

01:58:30.220 --> 01:58:34.690
for life and exploration is a good thing,

01:58:34.690 --> 01:58:36.490
then sure, behave in class.

01:58:36.490 --> 01:58:38.893
But when you get out of class, misbehave.

01:58:39.770 --> 01:58:41.500
You have my permission to do that.

01:58:41.500 --> 01:58:44.830
Do you realize in a few years
I'm gonna publish a book?

01:58:44.830 --> 01:58:46.983
Don't ask me about it until it comes out.

01:58:46.983 --> 01:58:48.220
I'm gonna publish a book,

01:58:48.220 --> 01:58:50.890
that will contain my
academic record in it.

01:58:50.890 --> 01:58:54.470
And it will contain
comments from my elementary

01:58:54.470 --> 01:58:59.260
school teachers, who complained constantly

01:58:59.260 --> 01:59:02.290
that my energy was disrupting the class.

01:59:02.290 --> 01:59:05.340
I was a bad student, for that reason.

01:59:05.340 --> 01:59:07.210
And I was not the teacher's pet.

01:59:07.210 --> 01:59:09.440
The teacher's pet was
the person who was quiet,

01:59:09.440 --> 01:59:12.160
got high grades, and
exactly obeyed everything

01:59:12.160 --> 01:59:14.490
the teacher said. And I had this energy,

01:59:14.490 --> 01:59:17.470
and I would crack jokes, and
I would show people stuff,

01:59:17.470 --> 01:59:21.610
and I would bring the urban
equivalent of a frog to class.

01:59:21.610 --> 01:59:24.370
(laughter)

01:59:24.370 --> 01:59:25.983
- Basically a rat?

01:59:30.048 --> 01:59:32.898
- So none of those teachers
would have said he'll go far.

01:59:33.740 --> 01:59:34.650
None of them.

01:59:34.650 --> 01:59:36.626
- Did you bring street rats to class?

01:59:36.626 --> 01:59:38.419
(laughter)

01:59:38.419 --> 01:59:39.510
Were you trying not to say that?

01:59:39.510 --> 01:59:41.315
- I was trying to figure out that too.

01:59:41.315 --> 01:59:42.213
- That's not cool.

01:59:43.620 --> 01:59:46.370
- So, like I said, you
will spend many more hours

01:59:46.370 --> 01:59:48.270
outside of school than in of school.

01:59:48.270 --> 01:59:51.310
So use the time outside
of school to misbehave

01:59:51.310 --> 01:59:54.150
all you want. And there's
the secret to becoming

01:59:54.150 --> 01:59:57.040
something greater than
the student who obeys

01:59:57.040 --> 01:59:59.335
every single command that
a teacher hands them.

01:59:59.335 --> 02:00:01.668
(applause)

02:00:03.810 --> 02:00:05.180
- Forgive me, but we have time only

02:00:05.180 --> 02:00:07.620
for one more question, I'm
gonna take right here, go.

02:00:07.620 --> 02:00:09.250
- I just wanna say to that young lady,

02:00:09.250 --> 02:00:10.790
can I just say real quick,
I'm sorry to interrupt.

02:00:10.790 --> 02:00:13.260
You don't have to go
back to the microphone.

02:00:13.260 --> 02:00:15.120
But look, there are a lot of times,

02:00:15.120 --> 02:00:17.290
even as a United States
senator where I have to

02:00:17.290 --> 02:00:20.750
shut up and be silent. And there are times

02:00:20.750 --> 02:00:23.537
when I don't want to be,
but my simple point to you,

02:00:23.537 --> 02:00:25.440
and you said it in the larger context.

02:00:25.440 --> 02:00:28.510
But allow someone authority, you have to

02:00:28.510 --> 02:00:31.747
to go through life, they
can silence your voice

02:00:31.747 --> 02:00:34.840
but never let them silence your spirit,

02:00:34.840 --> 02:00:39.227
that has got to continue,
to shout out loud.

02:00:39.227 --> 02:00:40.490
(applause)

02:00:40.490 --> 02:00:42.170
- Ladies and gentlemen, the
last question of the evening.

02:00:42.170 --> 02:00:43.550
I'm sorry for everyone else online.

02:00:43.550 --> 02:00:44.560
Yes, go.

02:00:44.560 --> 02:00:45.393
- [Audience Member] Hello.

02:00:45.393 --> 02:00:46.226
- Hello.

02:00:46.226 --> 02:00:48.540
- I wanted to ask, like, a
question about microbiology.

02:00:48.540 --> 02:00:49.494
Like something that you--

02:00:49.494 --> 02:00:50.327
- Yes.

02:00:51.860 --> 02:00:52.693
- There we go.

02:00:54.466 --> 02:00:58.120
- So I'm in an honors lab
bio class for freshman year,

02:00:58.120 --> 02:01:00.280
so we have a current
event that we have to do

02:01:00.280 --> 02:01:03.420
every 15th of the month, so I found one

02:01:03.420 --> 02:01:05.380
that had to do with bacteria in your mouth

02:01:05.380 --> 02:01:08.310
could have something
for your risk of cancer.

02:01:08.310 --> 02:01:10.850
And it mentioned microbiology in there

02:01:10.850 --> 02:01:13.430
and I wanted to know how could it affect

02:01:13.430 --> 02:01:15.883
cellular division and something like that.

02:01:17.290 --> 02:01:19.260
- Well, I don't think we
know the answer to that

02:01:19.260 --> 02:01:21.330
in that specific case, but there are a lot

02:01:21.330 --> 02:01:23.797
of proposals for how
bacteria would be influencing

02:01:23.797 --> 02:01:28.797
things like cell division
or cancer, or other events.

02:01:28.820 --> 02:01:30.740
And partly it's the things they produce.

02:01:30.740 --> 02:01:32.980
They produce chemicals,
and those chemicals

02:01:32.980 --> 02:01:36.450
can then induce cells
to do different things.

02:01:36.450 --> 02:01:38.880
And in other cases,
it's because they allow

02:01:38.880 --> 02:01:42.830
some other organism into the human system

02:01:42.830 --> 02:01:44.350
that then induces something else.

02:01:44.350 --> 02:01:46.470
So there are a lot of different
mechanisms, some of them

02:01:46.470 --> 02:01:50.640
not so direct. But we, I think,
are beginning to understand

02:01:50.640 --> 02:01:52.270
a little bit more about those.

02:01:52.270 --> 02:01:55.790
One that came out really
recently was really interesting

02:01:55.790 --> 02:01:58.700
that bacteria, they get out of the gut,

02:01:58.700 --> 02:02:02.580
sometimes because of
permeability in the wall,

02:02:02.580 --> 02:02:05.070
sometimes will incite an immune response

02:02:05.070 --> 02:02:08.275
that ends up killing a tumor.
So that's the kind of thing

02:02:08.275 --> 02:02:11.080
that nobody ever anticipated
as being a mechanism

02:02:11.080 --> 02:02:13.110
for suppressing cancer.

02:02:13.110 --> 02:02:15.067
- Also, recently, didn't you successfully,

02:02:15.067 --> 02:02:19.800
not you necessarily, successfully
create a poop transplant,

02:02:19.800 --> 02:02:21.044
fecal transplant?
- Yes.

02:02:21.044 --> 02:02:23.670
- Yes, I was hoping you'd talk
about the fecal transplant.

02:02:23.670 --> 02:02:24.894
Yo, hit 'em with it.

02:02:24.894 --> 02:02:27.148
(laughter)

02:02:27.148 --> 02:02:28.750
- You take poop out of
one person and you put it

02:02:28.750 --> 02:02:30.490
in another person and
it makes them better?

02:02:30.490 --> 02:02:31.323
- Yep.

02:02:31.323 --> 02:02:32.156
- That is nasty.

02:02:32.156 --> 02:02:32.989
- I've done it.

02:02:32.989 --> 02:02:35.044
(laughter)

02:02:37.020 --> 02:02:37.973
Not literally.

02:02:40.250 --> 02:02:43.430
- So these microbiomes are doing way more

02:02:43.430 --> 02:02:45.030
than we ever imagined.

02:02:45.030 --> 02:02:46.960
- Absolutely. And keeping us healthy

02:02:46.960 --> 02:02:48.950
is the biggest thing
they do. So remember that

02:02:48.950 --> 02:02:50.833
next time someone calls them germs.

02:02:52.220 --> 02:02:53.267
- We'll call them microbes.

02:02:53.267 --> 02:02:54.883
- Microbes. Good microbes.

02:02:54.883 --> 02:02:57.300
- Wasn't there a book
called "Microbes and Men?"

02:02:57.300 --> 02:02:58.278
- Yes.

02:02:58.278 --> 02:02:59.153
(laughter)

02:02:59.153 --> 02:03:02.516
But I think it was retitled
"Microbes and Women."

02:03:02.516 --> 02:03:04.187
(laughter)

02:03:04.187 --> 02:03:06.059
- "Microbes and Human Beings."

02:03:06.059 --> 02:03:06.892
- There you go.

02:03:06.892 --> 02:03:09.142
(applause)

02:03:15.580 --> 02:03:18.300
- So let me just go down the line here,

02:03:18.300 --> 02:03:19.870
before we close this out.

02:03:19.870 --> 02:03:21.473
And just, one by one if you could,

02:03:21.473 --> 02:03:25.530
what would be your recipe for
making America smart again?

02:03:25.530 --> 02:03:26.363
Just...

02:03:27.410 --> 02:03:28.243
Eugene.

02:03:28.243 --> 02:03:31.076
(laughter)

02:03:31.076 --> 02:03:33.661
(audience member yells)

02:03:33.661 --> 02:03:35.001
- I don't know what he said.

02:03:35.001 --> 02:03:37.480
(laughter)

02:03:37.480 --> 02:03:38.530
Yeah, I guess voting.

02:03:41.401 --> 02:03:42.366
- Voting.

02:03:42.366 --> 02:03:43.970
- Voting for science.
And also I think things

02:03:43.970 --> 02:03:45.732
like the Science March.

02:03:45.732 --> 02:03:48.046
(cheering)

02:03:48.046 --> 02:03:49.647
- The people that are
up there, they're going

02:03:49.647 --> 02:03:51.798
to the Science March.

02:03:51.798 --> 02:03:54.280
- I'm optimistic. In the end, I think

02:03:54.280 --> 02:03:56.960
that you can forward good things.

02:03:56.960 --> 02:04:00.253
And over time, it will work.

02:04:01.790 --> 02:04:03.290
So I think Science March is--

02:04:03.290 --> 02:04:04.437
- These immigrants are so hopeful.

02:04:04.437 --> 02:04:07.494
- I am very hopeful. I'm also sitting

02:04:07.494 --> 02:04:09.583
on a stage with you guys joking around.

02:04:10.720 --> 02:04:12.480
- Immigrants, they get the job done.

02:04:12.480 --> 02:04:15.050
- I believe in the
American dream. I adore it.

02:04:15.050 --> 02:04:16.180
- Ophira.

02:04:16.180 --> 02:04:19.600
- You know, I think we talk a lot about

02:04:19.600 --> 02:04:21.500
like right now, everyone's stressed out

02:04:21.500 --> 02:04:23.167
by what's going on, they're
like, "How am I gonna

02:04:23.167 --> 02:04:26.117
"deal with the climate? Self care. I'll go

02:04:26.117 --> 02:04:28.280
"to do some meditation and yoga."

02:04:28.280 --> 02:04:30.480
I think we have to stop
focusing on ourselves.

02:04:30.480 --> 02:04:32.988
I think we have to focus on other people

02:04:32.988 --> 02:04:36.290
and our community and think outside

02:04:36.290 --> 02:04:38.936
of ourselves more often, and think about

02:04:38.936 --> 02:04:42.700
how we are together,
rather than just laying

02:04:42.700 --> 02:04:45.037
down and going, "Ten minutes of head space

02:04:45.037 --> 02:04:47.128
"is gonna make it all better."

02:04:47.128 --> 02:04:47.961
- Okay.

02:04:47.961 --> 02:04:50.050
(applause)

02:04:50.050 --> 02:04:51.590
- John.

02:04:51.590 --> 02:04:53.920
- I'm gonna build on what Ophira has said,

02:04:53.920 --> 02:04:56.350
and suggest, as I've done elsewhere,

02:04:56.350 --> 02:05:00.440
that everybody who is in
science, in technology,

02:05:00.440 --> 02:05:02.860
or who cares about science and technology

02:05:02.860 --> 02:05:07.500
should tithe 10% of their time,
whatever else that they do.

02:05:07.500 --> 02:05:11.970
Tithe 10% of their time to
talking with other people,

02:05:11.970 --> 02:05:16.140
to engaging on how and why
science and technology matter

02:05:16.140 --> 02:05:19.040
to our society, to our
wellbeing, to the world.

02:05:19.040 --> 02:05:21.450
What science is, how it
works, what the sources

02:05:21.450 --> 02:05:24.500
of credibility in science
are, and why we need

02:05:24.500 --> 02:05:26.940
to preserve and protect science.

02:05:26.940 --> 02:05:29.590
We need all of us to
be better story tellers

02:05:29.590 --> 02:05:32.100
about this, to be activists, to be engaged

02:05:32.100 --> 02:05:33.976
in the policy process.

02:05:33.976 --> 02:05:36.226
(applause)

02:05:37.409 --> 02:05:38.409
- Baratunde.

02:05:41.541 --> 02:05:43.270
- Thank you for having me here.

02:05:43.270 --> 02:05:44.870
No I'm serious, it's
been an incredible honor

02:05:44.870 --> 02:05:47.140
to be on stage with these civil servants.

02:05:47.140 --> 02:05:49.580
Whether it's the comedic
arts, the arts and sciences,

02:05:49.580 --> 02:05:51.710
and I'm humbled to be a part of this.

02:05:51.710 --> 02:05:53.960
I wanna echo what Eugene
said. I happened to be

02:05:53.960 --> 02:05:57.230
at a meeting of the organizers
of the Science March.

02:05:57.230 --> 02:06:00.460
And one of them cited a Neils Bohr quote,

02:06:00.460 --> 02:06:03.210
quantum physics pioneer,
and I'm gonna paraphrase it,

02:06:03.210 --> 02:06:05.120
but he essentially described science

02:06:05.120 --> 02:06:08.100
as the steady reduction of prejudice.

02:06:08.100 --> 02:06:09.910
And if you think about
what science actually is,

02:06:09.910 --> 02:06:12.830
you constantly challenge
what you think to be true,

02:06:12.830 --> 02:06:15.416
and replace it to what
you know to be true.

02:06:15.416 --> 02:06:16.249
- Wow.

02:06:16.249 --> 02:06:17.082
- And if you're not
constantly challenging,

02:06:17.082 --> 02:06:18.400
that's not science.

02:06:18.400 --> 02:06:20.040
So we've been challenged up here,

02:06:20.040 --> 02:06:21.507
I encourage folks out there to do it.

02:06:21.507 --> 02:06:23.570
And I think what's coming
up with the Science March,

02:06:23.570 --> 02:06:25.600
whether it's you know,
before, during or after,

02:06:25.600 --> 02:06:27.180
is a testament to something much larger

02:06:27.180 --> 02:06:28.540
than the politics of the moment.

02:06:28.540 --> 02:06:30.420
It's about the larger pursuit of science,

02:06:30.420 --> 02:06:32.481
which is the reduction
of all of our prejudices.

02:06:32.481 --> 02:06:34.731
(applause)

02:06:37.794 --> 02:06:38.627
- Cory.

02:06:39.650 --> 02:06:41.930
- I guess I just would encourage people

02:06:41.930 --> 02:06:43.900
to, as Eugene said, to be people of hope.

02:06:43.900 --> 02:06:46.950
What I mean by that, is I
think this last 100 days

02:06:46.950 --> 02:06:50.180
has been some of the most
hopeful period in my time

02:06:50.180 --> 02:06:52.870
as a senator. And it's
not because the situation

02:06:52.870 --> 02:06:55.530
looks great, but I spent 8 years living

02:06:55.530 --> 02:06:58.060
in these high rise projects in Newark,

02:06:58.060 --> 02:06:59.620
and the tenant present
there who had her son

02:06:59.620 --> 02:07:02.800
murdered in the lobby of the
building which I lived in,

02:07:02.800 --> 02:07:05.042
she was one of the most
hopeful people that I met.

02:07:05.042 --> 02:07:07.730
And basically what she taught me was,

02:07:07.730 --> 02:07:09.880
that hope doesn't exist in the abstract.

02:07:09.880 --> 02:07:12.300
It's always a response to despair.

02:07:12.300 --> 02:07:15.700
It's saying that despair
will not have the last word.

02:07:15.700 --> 02:07:18.550
And that hope also is not a being word.

02:07:18.550 --> 02:07:20.540
You don't just sit in a
state of being that's hope.

02:07:20.540 --> 02:07:24.460
Hope is an active, it's a
fighter, it is constantly

02:07:24.460 --> 02:07:29.460
working to create that belief
that you haven't surrendered.

02:07:29.830 --> 02:07:33.780
And so my hope is that
I've seen the greatness

02:07:33.780 --> 02:07:35.870
of my country, whether
it was the Women's March

02:07:35.870 --> 02:07:38.400
or how the healthcare
bill, which was so awful,

02:07:38.400 --> 02:07:41.190
was beat back. Not by
politicians, but by a public,

02:07:41.190 --> 02:07:42.023
Republican and Democrat, who just said

02:07:42.023 --> 02:07:44.270
there is no way that
we're gonna tolerate that.

02:07:44.270 --> 02:07:48.001
And so right now, my prayer
is that everybody remembers

02:07:48.001 --> 02:07:50.350
those ten two letter words.

02:07:50.350 --> 02:07:52.700
That this country will succeed or fail

02:07:52.700 --> 02:07:54.820
based on those ten two letter words,

02:07:54.820 --> 02:07:56.220
and those ten two letter words are

02:07:56.220 --> 02:07:59.080
if it is to be it is
up to me. I have gotta

02:07:59.080 --> 02:08:02.864
be an agent of hope. And
that's for my part anyway.

02:08:02.864 --> 02:08:04.783
(applause)

02:08:04.783 --> 02:08:05.616
- Jo.

02:08:08.560 --> 02:08:10.550
- One of the things I
found really striking today

02:08:10.550 --> 02:08:13.560
was when we talked about
things that excite the public

02:08:13.560 --> 02:08:16.628
about science. It was
either because of fear

02:08:16.628 --> 02:08:20.770
or inspiration. And I
think we need to find a way

02:08:20.770 --> 02:08:25.770
to explain science and teach
science in logical ways,

02:08:26.194 --> 02:08:30.080
not fear mongering, but
that either insight fear

02:08:30.080 --> 02:08:32.850
or people's imaginations and inspiration.

02:08:32.850 --> 02:08:36.150
And it can't be just
discovering new planets,

02:08:36.150 --> 02:08:39.260
and discovering new cures.
It has to go way beyond

02:08:39.260 --> 02:08:42.130
that to all of science. And
I don't know how to do that.

02:08:42.130 --> 02:08:44.560
But I would challenge all
of us to think about that.

02:08:44.560 --> 02:08:47.970
How do we inspire people
about the fundamental quest

02:08:47.970 --> 02:08:50.913
for knowledge, which is
the basis for science.

02:08:50.913 --> 02:08:53.163
(applause)

02:08:58.681 --> 02:09:00.837
- All right if I could offer
some final reflections here.

02:09:00.837 --> 02:09:03.490
I mean, you guys said almost
anything I would have said.

02:09:03.490 --> 02:09:06.010
So, you really left me with nothing.

02:09:06.010 --> 02:09:07.410
I got nothing now.

02:09:07.410 --> 02:09:09.040
But let me share with you.

02:09:09.040 --> 02:09:12.190
Personally, I try not to have hope.

02:09:12.190 --> 02:09:14.770
Because hope is the confession

02:09:14.770 --> 02:09:17.900
that you have no control of the outcome.

02:09:17.900 --> 02:09:21.080
And I don't ever wanna
cede that to a word.

02:09:21.080 --> 02:09:25.147
I wanna say to myself,
"There's an outcome that I have

02:09:25.147 --> 02:09:28.150
"some access to, some control over."

02:09:28.150 --> 02:09:31.823
And lemme reiterate again,

02:09:32.661 --> 02:09:36.150
I don't beat back politicians.

02:09:36.150 --> 02:09:38.240
There's something else deeper than that.

02:09:38.240 --> 02:09:41.170
In our K through 12 system, what do we do?

02:09:41.170 --> 02:09:44.920
I think we view students as these vessels.

02:09:44.920 --> 02:09:48.200
Where you unzip their brain, their head,

02:09:48.200 --> 02:09:51.580
and pour information in for 12 years.

02:09:51.580 --> 02:09:54.050
And then you zip it back
up, hand them the diploma,

02:09:54.050 --> 02:09:55.530
and send them off.

02:09:55.530 --> 02:09:59.560
And so we think that being
educated is knowing stuff.

02:09:59.560 --> 02:10:03.200
When somewhere in there,
one ought to be taught

02:10:03.200 --> 02:10:08.200
how to question knowledge,
how to evaluate information

02:10:09.170 --> 02:10:12.440
and evidence. These are
the foundations of science.

02:10:12.440 --> 02:10:14.200
We don't even have to call it science.

02:10:14.200 --> 02:10:18.270
Let's just call it curiosity,
because what is a scientist

02:10:18.270 --> 02:10:22.673
but a kid who had never really grew up.

02:10:22.673 --> 02:10:25.360
All right. It's a kid, who in adulthood,

02:10:25.360 --> 02:10:28.190
retained child-like curiosity.

02:10:28.190 --> 02:10:30.380
And when you retain child-like curiosity,

02:10:30.380 --> 02:10:33.630
anything that happens before
you is up for questioning.

02:10:33.630 --> 02:10:35.860
And you say, well why are
you doing it that way?

02:10:35.860 --> 02:10:36.950
Can't it happen this way?

02:10:36.950 --> 02:10:38.550
Well let me research that.

02:10:38.550 --> 02:10:42.040
And if you, in addition to being trained

02:10:42.040 --> 02:10:44.716
how to think about information,

02:10:44.716 --> 02:10:48.140
if somehow we can retain your curiosity,

02:10:48.140 --> 02:10:50.850
from childhood through adulthood,

02:10:50.850 --> 02:10:54.680
retain that curiosity, then
you become lifelong learners.

02:10:54.680 --> 02:10:57.510
Lifelong inquisitors.
Because we will spend

02:10:57.510 --> 02:11:00.730
many more years outside
of school than in school.

02:11:00.730 --> 02:11:03.390
How many people do we know,
if not among ourselves,

02:11:03.390 --> 02:11:05.190
the last day of school you take your books

02:11:05.190 --> 02:11:08.130
and throw them into the air
and say, "School's done."

02:11:08.130 --> 02:11:11.570
As though that's the
state you want to be in,

02:11:11.570 --> 02:11:14.070
where you no longer have to learn.

02:11:14.070 --> 02:11:15.740
That's a failure of
the educational system.

02:11:15.740 --> 02:11:17.447
You should come outta school
saying, "Gee, I'm still

02:11:17.447 --> 02:11:20.867
"curious, can I go back
in? Or are there some ways

02:11:20.867 --> 02:11:21.810
"I can keep learning?"

02:11:21.810 --> 02:11:25.120
And I think that if we
breed an entire generation

02:11:25.120 --> 02:11:28.100
of people that are curious into adulthood,

02:11:28.100 --> 02:11:30.806
then you will never elect someone

02:11:30.806 --> 02:11:33.803
who just states things that are not true.

02:11:33.803 --> 02:11:35.407
That would never happen.

02:11:35.407 --> 02:11:37.657
(applause)

02:11:44.170 --> 02:11:48.180
You would build into the system curiosity.

02:11:48.180 --> 02:11:49.560
And where does the politic turn?

02:11:49.560 --> 02:11:51.943
The politics layers on top of that.

02:11:52.800 --> 02:11:54.670
So you don't say, "There
is no global warming."

02:11:54.670 --> 02:11:56.780
We know there is.

02:11:56.780 --> 02:11:58.620
So now that we know there is,

02:11:58.620 --> 02:12:01.000
let's have the political conversation.

02:12:01.000 --> 02:12:02.383
Are there carbon credits?

02:12:05.073 --> 02:12:07.270
Do you subsidize?

02:12:07.270 --> 02:12:08.840
Or do you put up tariffs?

02:12:08.840 --> 02:12:12.010
That's where the politics needs to happen.

02:12:12.010 --> 02:12:14.490
Not at any level below that.

02:12:14.490 --> 02:12:16.890
So my sense of this is, you wanna

02:12:16.890 --> 02:12:20.480
make America great, you
first have to make it smart,

02:12:20.480 --> 02:12:22.640
and to make it smart, we have to retain

02:12:22.640 --> 02:12:25.400
the curiosity that we all had as children.

02:12:25.400 --> 02:12:27.900
And that way, we can
turn a sleepy country,

02:12:27.900 --> 02:12:29.960
into an innovation nation.

02:12:29.960 --> 02:12:30.932
- Amen.

02:12:30.932 --> 02:12:33.182
(applause)

02:12:36.170 --> 02:12:39.873
- Red Bank, New Jersey,
this has been StarTalk!

02:12:39.873 --> 02:12:42.806
And I've been your host,
Neil DeGrasse Tyson,

02:12:42.806 --> 02:12:43.960
and my guests.

02:12:43.960 --> 02:12:47.663
Baratunde, the senator, Jo, John,

02:12:49.258 --> 02:12:52.800
Ophira, Eugene.

02:12:52.800 --> 02:12:54.872
Thank you all New Jersey!

02:12:54.872 --> 02:12:57.122
(applause)

02:13:00.590 --> 02:13:02.963
As always, keep looking up.

02:13:05.981 --> 02:13:08.648
(upbeat music)

